1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,093

2
00:00:05,093 --> 00:00:07,720
All right, let's get started.

3
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:08,620
Great.

4
00:00:08,620 --> 00:00:11,280
So for today, we're planning
to finish offline reinforcement

5
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:11,980
learning.

6
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:13,880
And along the reward
learning front,

7
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:16,960
the key goals today
are to figure out

8
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:19,800
why is task specification hard.

9
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:24,400
Why do naive methods for
trying to specify rewards fail?

10
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:28,000
And what are some methods
for learning reward functions

11
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,440
from human supervision?

12
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:31,980
Cool.

13
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:36,280
So to start off with finishing
offline reinforcement learning,

14
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:39,160
we talked about this
problem on Wednesday

15
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:41,320
where our goal is
to learn a policy

16
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:45,680
from a batch of existing
data without any online data

17
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:46,920
collection.

18
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:49,840
And we assume that the
batch of offline data

19
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:52,520
is collected by
some unknown policy

20
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:56,400
that we refer to as the
behavior policy or pi beta.

21
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:59,840
And our goal is to maximize
rewards under pi theta.

22
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:02,500
And so we have this challenge
of a distribution shift

23
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:05,780
between this prior
policy that collected

24
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:10,420
the data and the policy
that we're optimizing.

25
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:12,240
One of the challenges
that comes up,

26
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:14,860
if you try to use traditional
reinforcement learning

27
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:17,740
algorithms in this setting, is
that-- if, for example, you're

28
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:22,380
trying to optimize a Q function
to then improve your policy,

29
00:01:22,380 --> 00:01:26,620
then you might query your
Q function on actions

30
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:28,480
that were sampled from
your learned policy,

31
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:31,540
and those actions might
not be covered in the data.

32
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:33,420
And that can cause
overestimation.

33
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:37,020
And it can cause your Q
function to be inaccurate.

34
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:38,460
We talked about
this picture where

35
00:01:38,460 --> 00:01:41,540
if we had randomly
initialized some Q function,

36
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:47,100
and the data only covers actions
along this range for this given

37
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:50,140
state, then we might--

38
00:01:50,140 --> 00:01:52,140
then when we try
to actually learn

39
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:54,160
a policy that optimizes
the Q function,

40
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:55,860
then it will start
to query actions

41
00:01:55,860 --> 00:02:00,320
that are over here because they
randomly have a higher Q value.

42
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:04,880
And that's one of the key
challenges in offline RL.

43
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:07,521
Now we talked about
one algorithm that--

44
00:02:07,521 --> 00:02:08,979
I talked about a
couple algorithms.

45
00:02:08,979 --> 00:02:11,187
But the main algorithm that
we talked about last time

46
00:02:11,187 --> 00:02:12,880
was this IQL algorithm.

47
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:15,680
And IQL addresses the
offline RL problem

48
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:20,800
by actually avoiding
any sort of queries,

49
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:24,080
any sort of function queries
beyond the data set actions

50
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:25,640
altogether.

51
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:32,880
And it did this by actually
just learning a value function

52
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:36,960
and a Q function using the
states and actions in the data.

53
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:40,300
Now if you just do that
with the normal updates,

54
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:44,665
then you'll learn V pi and Q
pi for the behavior policy.

55
00:02:44,665 --> 00:02:47,040
So you'll learn a value function
for the behavior policy.

56
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:49,600
And that won't allow
you to improve very much

57
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:52,280
over the behavior policy.

58
00:02:52,280 --> 00:02:54,880
And so instead, when
learning the value function,

59
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:58,810
it used this
asymmetric loss to try

60
00:02:58,810 --> 00:03:01,970
to learn a value function
for a policy that's better

61
00:03:01,970 --> 00:03:04,770
than the behavior policy.

62
00:03:04,770 --> 00:03:08,970
And then it learns both the
value function and a Q function.

63
00:03:08,970 --> 00:03:12,550
For the Q function, it
uses a standard L2 loss.

64
00:03:12,550 --> 00:03:15,330
One thing I didn't quite
go over with regard to IQL

65
00:03:15,330 --> 00:03:18,390
is why use an asymmetric
loss for the value function,

66
00:03:18,390 --> 00:03:20,090
but not for the Q function.

67
00:03:20,090 --> 00:03:23,090
And one way to think
about that is you

68
00:03:23,090 --> 00:03:25,820
can think about when
you're taking actions--

69
00:03:25,820 --> 00:03:28,150
when you're rolling
out a policy,

70
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:30,010
there's two sources
of variability.

71
00:03:30,010 --> 00:03:32,690
One is that the different
actions that you might take.

72
00:03:32,690 --> 00:03:36,410
And one is given an action,
the state that you end up in.

73
00:03:36,410 --> 00:03:40,170
And if you think
about, for example,

74
00:03:40,170 --> 00:03:45,130
the Q function after taking
an action and separately

75
00:03:45,130 --> 00:03:51,490
the value function of the
state that comes after that,

76
00:03:51,490 --> 00:03:52,790
these might be different.

77
00:03:52,790 --> 00:03:54,830
So because you actually--
because there's

78
00:03:54,830 --> 00:03:56,950
randomness in the
environment, the value

79
00:03:56,950 --> 00:04:00,647
function for the next state,
maybe it's something like 8,

80
00:04:00,647 --> 00:04:02,230
because maybe you
got somewhat unlucky

81
00:04:02,230 --> 00:04:03,590
in terms of the next state.

82
00:04:03,590 --> 00:04:07,590
And it might be less
than the Q function

83
00:04:07,590 --> 00:04:09,830
after taking this action.

84
00:04:09,830 --> 00:04:14,350
And likewise for these
other states and actions,

85
00:04:14,350 --> 00:04:15,690
you might have some variability.

86
00:04:15,690 --> 00:04:20,350
So maybe Q of (s, a2).

87
00:04:20,350 --> 00:04:22,710
Maybe that's equal
to 5, because this is

88
00:04:22,710 --> 00:04:25,390
an action that's worse than a1.

89
00:04:25,390 --> 00:04:28,790
Whereas perhaps
when you actually

90
00:04:28,790 --> 00:04:30,630
rolled out a2 in
the environment,

91
00:04:30,630 --> 00:04:36,190
the subsequent state
after taking that action

92
00:04:36,190 --> 00:04:39,470
has a true value of--

93
00:04:39,470 --> 00:04:40,570
maybe a true value of 8.

94
00:04:40,570 --> 00:04:42,910
Maybe you got lucky this time.

95
00:04:42,910 --> 00:04:44,710
Maybe this action is really bad.

96
00:04:44,710 --> 00:04:50,590
In that case, then
Q hat (s, a3).

97
00:04:50,590 --> 00:04:52,110
Maybe it was a
very risky action,

98
00:04:52,110 --> 00:04:55,370
so it has a key
value of negative 5.

99
00:04:55,370 --> 00:04:58,130
And then maybe when you
actually took that risky action

100
00:04:58,130 --> 00:05:00,730
in the environment, maybe
it wasn't quite as bad

101
00:05:00,730 --> 00:05:02,510
as what could have happened.

102
00:05:02,510 --> 00:05:07,170
And so in that case, the
value for that next state

103
00:05:07,170 --> 00:05:09,450
maybe is something
like 0 because it

104
00:05:09,450 --> 00:05:11,113
didn't turn out as bad.

105
00:05:11,113 --> 00:05:13,530
And the reason why I'm writing
out these different numbers

106
00:05:13,530 --> 00:05:16,650
is that we can think about
the asymmetric loss function

107
00:05:16,650 --> 00:05:19,130
when learning the value for IQL.

108
00:05:19,130 --> 00:05:22,930
And the first loss function
when learning the value

109
00:05:22,930 --> 00:05:25,830
is trying to regress to
the different Q values.

110
00:05:25,830 --> 00:05:27,970
And we could either
learn the average Q value

111
00:05:27,970 --> 00:05:32,470
or try to lean towards
the larger Q values.

112
00:05:32,470 --> 00:05:34,550
And if we lean towards
the larger Q values,

113
00:05:34,550 --> 00:05:37,592
that corresponds to
actions that are better.

114
00:05:37,592 --> 00:05:39,050
And so that
corresponds to a policy

115
00:05:39,050 --> 00:05:42,490
that's better than
the behavior policy.

116
00:05:42,490 --> 00:05:45,890
On the contrary, if we instead
use an asymmetric loss function

117
00:05:45,890 --> 00:05:48,930
in the Q function, the
targets for the Q function

118
00:05:48,930 --> 00:05:51,390
correspond to the next value.

119
00:05:51,390 --> 00:05:52,590
And if we instead--

120
00:05:52,590 --> 00:05:54,550
if we use this L2
loss, we'll just

121
00:05:54,550 --> 00:05:57,390
be optimizing for the
average value here.

122
00:05:57,390 --> 00:06:00,110
If we instead use
an asymmetric loss,

123
00:06:00,110 --> 00:06:03,750
then we'd be trying to
regress to the future state

124
00:06:03,750 --> 00:06:06,430
values that are higher,
which would actually

125
00:06:06,430 --> 00:06:11,470
be optimizing for not a better
policy, but a more lucky policy.

126
00:06:11,470 --> 00:06:15,710
And that's something that you
can't control with your actions.

127
00:06:15,710 --> 00:06:17,750
Yeah, so in the IQL
objective, we're

128
00:06:17,750 --> 00:06:21,390
actually training both the
value function and a Q function.

129
00:06:21,390 --> 00:06:26,350
And the reason for that is
because we want to be finding--

130
00:06:26,350 --> 00:06:33,190
the goal is to fit V
hat pi for a pi that

131
00:06:33,190 --> 00:06:36,830
is better than the
behavior policy

132
00:06:36,830 --> 00:06:41,510
or better than the policy
that underlies the data set.

133
00:06:41,510 --> 00:06:44,630
And as we see
here, if we do that

134
00:06:44,630 --> 00:06:49,530
by regressing to these Q
values, then we will pick--

135
00:06:49,530 --> 00:06:51,950
we'll lean towards--
instead of the average,

136
00:06:51,950 --> 00:06:54,790
lean towards the key values that
correspond to better actions.

137
00:06:54,790 --> 00:06:57,265
And so that's learning
a value function

138
00:06:57,265 --> 00:06:58,390
for a policy that's better.

139
00:06:58,390 --> 00:07:01,850
This takes better actions
than the behavior policy.

140
00:07:01,850 --> 00:07:04,370
Now, we can't just use--

141
00:07:04,370 --> 00:07:07,230
it's useful to have a Q function
and not just a value function,

142
00:07:07,230 --> 00:07:09,970
because the Q function is
what helps us figure out

143
00:07:09,970 --> 00:07:13,930
which actions are
better when we use

144
00:07:13,930 --> 00:07:15,790
advantage-weighted
regression and so forth.

145
00:07:15,790 --> 00:07:18,690
And so we also learn
a Q function as well.

146
00:07:18,690 --> 00:07:20,530
But for that, we
use asymmetric loss

147
00:07:20,530 --> 00:07:23,810
so that we're not
optimizing for being lucky.

148
00:07:23,810 --> 00:07:25,930
OK.

149
00:07:25,930 --> 00:07:31,690
So IQL was one way to learn a
policy from an offline data set.

150
00:07:31,690 --> 00:07:34,670
There's also some
other ways to do it.

151
00:07:34,670 --> 00:07:37,290
And in particular, we'll talk
about one more way, one more

152
00:07:37,290 --> 00:07:38,570
algorithm.

153
00:07:38,570 --> 00:07:40,850
And in this case,
we're still going

154
00:07:40,850 --> 00:07:42,930
to try to estimate the value.

155
00:07:42,930 --> 00:07:46,950
We're still going to actually
query actions from our policy.

156
00:07:46,950 --> 00:07:50,510
And we want to think about how
to discourage overestimation

157
00:07:50,510 --> 00:07:55,590
and discourage the RL
algorithm from taking actions

158
00:07:55,590 --> 00:07:59,550
that you think might have high
Q value because they're out

159
00:07:59,550 --> 00:08:01,990
of distribution.

160
00:08:01,990 --> 00:08:07,210
And so one idea is, what if we
just outside of the data support

161
00:08:07,210 --> 00:08:10,230
try to minimize the Q values?

162
00:08:10,230 --> 00:08:12,230
Can we just basically
take this part

163
00:08:12,230 --> 00:08:15,030
and try to push down
on the Q values that

164
00:08:15,030 --> 00:08:17,830
are out of distribution?

165
00:08:17,830 --> 00:08:19,830
And if we did
that, then we would

166
00:08:19,830 --> 00:08:22,830
be pessimistic or
conservative with regard

167
00:08:22,830 --> 00:08:27,590
to everything that's out
of distribution and not

168
00:08:27,590 --> 00:08:29,070
as conservative
when we're actually

169
00:08:29,070 --> 00:08:32,190
in the support of the data.

170
00:08:32,190 --> 00:08:38,429
And so we could formulate some
loss function that does this.

171
00:08:38,429 --> 00:08:45,080
And what this would
look like is you're

172
00:08:45,080 --> 00:08:46,920
trying to fit a Q function.

173
00:08:46,920 --> 00:08:55,180
And you will have your
standard loss for your critic,

174
00:08:55,180 --> 00:08:56,180
for your value function.

175
00:08:56,180 --> 00:08:58,680
I'll call this L critic.

176
00:08:58,680 --> 00:09:01,520
Then we could also
add a loss function.

177
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:07,620
This is for-- we're trying
to fit the Q function.

178
00:09:07,620 --> 00:09:10,720
So maybe that Q function
has parameters phi.

179
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:12,600
And then we could add
an additional term

180
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:16,400
that is trying to
additionally minimize

181
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:20,200
the key values on actions
that are out of distribution.

182
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:26,880
So we would try to be
minimizing our Q values.

183
00:09:26,880 --> 00:09:30,520
And in particular, we want
to minimize key values

184
00:09:30,520 --> 00:09:32,280
for our distribution actions.

185
00:09:32,280 --> 00:09:36,440
And so this would
be an expectation

186
00:09:36,440 --> 00:09:43,700
over states sampled from
your data set and actions

187
00:09:43,700 --> 00:09:45,712
sampled from some other
distribution that's

188
00:09:45,712 --> 00:09:46,420
not the data set.

189
00:09:46,420 --> 00:09:49,180

190
00:09:49,180 --> 00:09:51,100
And ideally, this
distribution would

191
00:09:51,100 --> 00:09:53,420
cover the
out-of-distribution actions

192
00:09:53,420 --> 00:09:56,000
that your policy
might be considering.

193
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,340

194
00:09:59,340 --> 00:10:02,260
So this is some
other distribution.

195
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:07,060
That distribution would probably
be a function of [? f. ?]

196
00:10:07,060 --> 00:10:09,260
Now if you want to
think about which

197
00:10:09,260 --> 00:10:11,977
out-of-distribution actions your
policy might be considering,

198
00:10:11,977 --> 00:10:13,560
your policy is going
to be considering

199
00:10:13,560 --> 00:10:18,100
the out-of-distribution
actions that have high Q value.

200
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:24,700
And so one initial version
that we could do here

201
00:10:24,700 --> 00:10:29,180
is we could pick a mu,
pick this distribution,

202
00:10:29,180 --> 00:10:33,660
such that it has high Q
values, because our policy will

203
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:37,180
be trying to take actions
that have high Q values.

204
00:10:37,180 --> 00:10:42,160
And so what this would look
like is minimizing our critic

205
00:10:42,160 --> 00:10:48,200
parameters and
choosing a mu such

206
00:10:48,200 --> 00:10:53,257
that it's maximizing
the Q values.

207
00:10:53,257 --> 00:10:54,840
So what this will
do is it will have--

208
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:57,240
it will try to optimize
a critic and then

209
00:10:57,240 --> 00:11:02,520
minimize the Q
values for actions

210
00:11:02,520 --> 00:11:06,480
that have a high Q value.

211
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:07,610
Now this is version 1.

212
00:11:07,610 --> 00:11:10,600

213
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:13,240
We're going to be pushing
down on large Q values.

214
00:11:13,240 --> 00:11:17,600
You can show with this
version that if you have

215
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:19,652
a large enough alpha, if you--

216
00:11:19,652 --> 00:11:23,200
you don't necessarily-- you
want to have some sort of weight

217
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:24,020
on this objective.

218
00:11:24,020 --> 00:11:25,460
This is going to be
a hyperparameter.

219
00:11:25,460 --> 00:11:26,835
If you have a
large enough alpha,

220
00:11:26,835 --> 00:11:30,000
you can show that the critic
that you learned from this

221
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:35,520
is going to be a lower bound
on the true Q function.

222
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:39,660
And so then what this
would look like is

223
00:11:39,660 --> 00:11:46,140
if you have some plot
of your Q functions

224
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:50,220
for a different action, and
if your data support is maybe

225
00:11:50,220 --> 00:11:52,860
something like this,
something that you might learn

226
00:11:52,860 --> 00:11:57,320
is a lower bound on
the true Q function,

227
00:11:57,320 --> 00:12:00,320
where this latter one
is going to be Q hat.

228
00:12:00,320 --> 00:12:04,380

229
00:12:04,380 --> 00:12:06,500
Now, one downside
with this is that you

230
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:10,778
might be a little
bit too pessimistic,

231
00:12:10,778 --> 00:12:12,820
because this is actually
going to be pushing down

232
00:12:12,820 --> 00:12:14,540
on all Q values.

233
00:12:14,540 --> 00:12:16,960
But we know that
for the Q values,

234
00:12:16,960 --> 00:12:19,740
for the actions in the data
set, for the part that's

235
00:12:19,740 --> 00:12:26,300
in the support of our
data set, these actions

236
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:28,015
we have more data for.

237
00:12:28,015 --> 00:12:30,140
And so we don't necessarily
want to be pushing down

238
00:12:30,140 --> 00:12:34,203
on the key values
for those actions.

239
00:12:34,203 --> 00:12:35,620
And so we could
do is we could add

240
00:12:35,620 --> 00:12:40,420
a term that basically cancels
out this term for the data

241
00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:42,240
sets in the actions.

242
00:12:42,240 --> 00:12:44,120
And so what that
would do is just--

243
00:12:44,120 --> 00:12:47,240
what that would
look like is simply

244
00:12:47,240 --> 00:12:51,840
taking this same exact
term and subtracting it

245
00:12:51,840 --> 00:12:59,160
for states in the data set, but
also actions in the data set.

246
00:12:59,160 --> 00:13:03,840
So we're going to be minimizing
the Q values for actions

247
00:13:03,840 --> 00:13:06,360
from this distribution
and maximizing

248
00:13:06,360 --> 00:13:09,040
for actions in the data set.

249
00:13:09,040 --> 00:13:13,760
And this will hopefully-- the
goal of this would be to get a Q

250
00:13:13,760 --> 00:13:16,920
function that inside the
support of the data--

251
00:13:16,920 --> 00:13:18,440
it's not a very good marker.

252
00:13:18,440 --> 00:13:22,120
That's inside the support of
the data is maybe very accurate.

253
00:13:22,120 --> 00:13:25,253
And then for the things
outside the support of the data

254
00:13:25,253 --> 00:13:27,318
has a low Q values,
because we know

255
00:13:27,318 --> 00:13:28,860
that outside the
support of the data,

256
00:13:28,860 --> 00:13:31,662
we aren't going to be able to
learn an accurate Q function.

257
00:13:31,662 --> 00:13:33,120
Yeah, so the question
is, does this

258
00:13:33,120 --> 00:13:35,620
affect your ability to
generalize outside the support

259
00:13:35,620 --> 00:13:36,620
of the data?

260
00:13:36,620 --> 00:13:39,180
So certainly, it does.

261
00:13:39,180 --> 00:13:42,020
It's going to cause you
to be basically more

262
00:13:42,020 --> 00:13:44,410
pessimistic outside of
the support of the data.

263
00:13:44,410 --> 00:13:49,620

264
00:13:49,620 --> 00:13:51,580
This hyperparameter
alpha is going

265
00:13:51,580 --> 00:13:53,460
to control how
pessimistic you are.

266
00:13:53,460 --> 00:13:55,340
And if you think that you're
in a regime where you might

267
00:13:55,340 --> 00:13:56,923
be able to generalize
better, then you

268
00:13:56,923 --> 00:13:59,180
could choose an
alpha that is smaller

269
00:13:59,180 --> 00:14:02,180
so that you're using more of
the original critic objective

270
00:14:02,180 --> 00:14:06,380
and less of this
regularization term.

271
00:14:06,380 --> 00:14:07,780
Yeah.

272
00:14:07,780 --> 00:14:11,420
The question is, should
it be Q or Q hat?

273
00:14:11,420 --> 00:14:11,920
Right.

274
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:17,360
So it should be Q hat phi.

275
00:14:17,360 --> 00:14:20,166
So this is our--

276
00:14:20,166 --> 00:14:23,220
this is the Q function that
we're learning right here.

277
00:14:23,220 --> 00:14:25,580
The notation on the slide
is slightly different.

278
00:14:25,580 --> 00:14:27,040
Sorry for the difference there.

279
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:29,660
So on the slides, we're
treating Q as the thing

280
00:14:29,660 --> 00:14:32,940
that we're minimizing.

281
00:14:32,940 --> 00:14:36,230
And if we view it instead of
minimizing these parameters,

282
00:14:36,230 --> 00:14:38,510
then this is how
we would write it.

283
00:14:38,510 --> 00:14:40,750
Yeah, so the question
is, what is mu?

284
00:14:40,750 --> 00:14:43,870
Is it this fake policy
that's outside the support?

285
00:14:43,870 --> 00:14:48,950
You can think of mu as
sort of as a policy.

286
00:14:48,950 --> 00:14:51,450
It's not actually the policy
that we're going to be running,

287
00:14:51,450 --> 00:14:54,350
but it's essentially going to be
something that is trying to seek

288
00:14:54,350 --> 00:14:57,870
out the Q values that have--

289
00:14:57,870 --> 00:14:59,350
the Q values that--
or the actions

290
00:14:59,350 --> 00:15:01,670
that have high Q value so
that if, for example, you're

291
00:15:01,670 --> 00:15:06,093
over here, you would minimize
the key values there.

292
00:15:06,093 --> 00:15:07,510
And yeah, it's
basically something

293
00:15:07,510 --> 00:15:10,067
that's trying to seek
out large key values,

294
00:15:10,067 --> 00:15:11,650
either in or outside
the distribution.

295
00:15:11,650 --> 00:15:13,250
Once it gets inside
the distribution,

296
00:15:13,250 --> 00:15:17,530
you're then going to be
canceling out with this term.

297
00:15:17,530 --> 00:15:19,550
And so then you'll have
a more accurate estimate

298
00:15:19,550 --> 00:15:21,630
inside the support of the data.

299
00:15:21,630 --> 00:15:24,857
The question is, does it mop
out the Q for the actions

300
00:15:24,857 --> 00:15:25,690
outside the support.

301
00:15:25,690 --> 00:15:27,840
And yeah, that's
basically what it's doing.

302
00:15:27,840 --> 00:15:29,530
Oh, in terms of--
the question is,

303
00:15:29,530 --> 00:15:32,150
what does it mean to maximize
mu in terms of the distribution?

304
00:15:32,150 --> 00:15:35,870
It basically means that we're
going to be picking a mu such

305
00:15:35,870 --> 00:15:38,170
that it has high Q values.

306
00:15:38,170 --> 00:15:40,850
In practice, we'll
actually see in a minute.

307
00:15:40,850 --> 00:15:43,410
We can actually not have
to explicitly represent

308
00:15:43,410 --> 00:15:47,570
mu for some choices, but we'll
talk about that in a second.

309
00:15:47,570 --> 00:15:49,450
The question is, if
our goal is to minimize

310
00:15:49,450 --> 00:15:51,950
Q for things outside the
training distribution,

311
00:15:51,950 --> 00:15:56,050
why not just do normal
behavior cloning?

312
00:15:56,050 --> 00:15:58,290
So one of the things that's
nice about this algorithm

313
00:15:58,290 --> 00:16:01,110
is it has the potential to do
better than behavior cloning.

314
00:16:01,110 --> 00:16:04,570
So any sort of RL method
will be leveraging the reward

315
00:16:04,570 --> 00:16:07,825
information to learn a policy
that has high Q values.

316
00:16:07,825 --> 00:16:09,450
And so you can actually
use this method

317
00:16:09,450 --> 00:16:13,350
to do considerably better
than behavior cloning.

318
00:16:13,350 --> 00:16:14,850
And then we also
talked on Wednesday

319
00:16:14,850 --> 00:16:17,475
about a method that can also do
better by behavior cloning just

320
00:16:17,475 --> 00:16:18,650
by ranking things.

321
00:16:18,650 --> 00:16:24,050
Generally, methods that try
to train the policy on only

322
00:16:24,050 --> 00:16:26,250
the actions in the
distribution are generally

323
00:16:26,250 --> 00:16:28,090
a valid way to do offline RL.

324
00:16:28,090 --> 00:16:31,830
This is thinking about an
alternative way to do it.

325
00:16:31,830 --> 00:16:33,030
OK.

326
00:16:33,030 --> 00:16:36,990
So in terms of--

327
00:16:36,990 --> 00:16:38,612
when we introduced
this last term,

328
00:16:38,612 --> 00:16:40,070
we're actually no
longer guaranteed

329
00:16:40,070 --> 00:16:43,030
to have a lower bound on the
Q function for all states

330
00:16:43,030 --> 00:16:44,050
and actions.

331
00:16:44,050 --> 00:16:47,270
But we're actually
guaranteed an expectation

332
00:16:47,270 --> 00:16:52,430
to have a lower bound for all
the states in our data set.

333
00:16:52,430 --> 00:16:54,670
I'm not going to go through
the derivation for this,

334
00:16:54,670 --> 00:17:00,230
but it's a nice property
to have that you

335
00:17:00,230 --> 00:17:07,109
will get some sort of lower
bound on the true Q function.

336
00:17:07,109 --> 00:17:13,470
And it suggests that it's a
reasonable objective to use.

337
00:17:13,470 --> 00:17:15,151
[? If ?] alpha is 0,
it does not hold--

338
00:17:15,151 --> 00:17:16,609
alpha does need to
be large enough.

339
00:17:16,609 --> 00:17:19,150
I can't remember the
exact conditions on alpha.

340
00:17:19,150 --> 00:17:24,890
But you could take a look at
the CQL paper for the proof.

341
00:17:24,890 --> 00:17:25,390
Cool.

342
00:17:25,390 --> 00:17:29,650
So this is an algorithm called
conservative Q-learning.

343
00:17:29,650 --> 00:17:31,530
In terms of the
full algorithm, we

344
00:17:31,530 --> 00:17:36,450
will optimize this objective
using our data set.

345
00:17:36,450 --> 00:17:41,330
And then we'll get a Q
function as a result of this.

346
00:17:41,330 --> 00:17:45,590
And then after we
optimize our Q function,

347
00:17:45,590 --> 00:17:48,730
we can then update
our policy and then

348
00:17:48,730 --> 00:17:52,450
repeat that process to--

349
00:17:52,450 --> 00:17:55,530
and yeah.

350
00:17:55,530 --> 00:18:01,970
If the actions are discrete,
then you can just in some ways

351
00:18:01,970 --> 00:18:04,650
read out the actions by
picking the actions that

352
00:18:04,650 --> 00:18:07,450
have maximum probability.

353
00:18:07,450 --> 00:18:09,370
If they're continuous,
then you'll

354
00:18:09,370 --> 00:18:13,710
need to use some sort of
update to optimize your policy.

355
00:18:13,710 --> 00:18:16,210
And you'll be wanting to
optimize your policy such

356
00:18:16,210 --> 00:18:19,550
that you are picking actions
that have high Q value.

357
00:18:19,550 --> 00:18:24,370

358
00:18:24,370 --> 00:18:25,210
Cool.

359
00:18:25,210 --> 00:18:27,190
So we talked about this mu term.

360
00:18:27,190 --> 00:18:31,070
And in practice, if we had
to construct this mu term,

361
00:18:31,070 --> 00:18:34,470
that would be annoying, and
it would add a moving part

362
00:18:34,470 --> 00:18:37,030
to the algorithm.

363
00:18:37,030 --> 00:18:43,900
And so in terms of thinking
what this mu should be,

364
00:18:43,900 --> 00:18:48,110
you want to pick a mu that is
going to actually have high Q

365
00:18:48,110 --> 00:18:52,310
values, because that would
allow it to push down

366
00:18:52,310 --> 00:18:54,763
erroneously large Q values.

367
00:18:54,763 --> 00:18:56,930
And when you apply it on
things with small Q values,

368
00:18:56,930 --> 00:19:00,150
it's not really going to
have a meaningful effect.

369
00:19:00,150 --> 00:19:04,175
But you also don't want to--

370
00:19:04,175 --> 00:19:06,170
you want it to--

371
00:19:06,170 --> 00:19:08,950
you want it to be considering
actions that are not just

372
00:19:08,950 --> 00:19:12,330
like exactly the
one largest Q value.

373
00:19:12,330 --> 00:19:15,510
And as written, this
mu term might actually

374
00:19:15,510 --> 00:19:17,750
collapse to be
very deterministic

375
00:19:17,750 --> 00:19:22,710
and just pick the one action
that has the largest Q value.

376
00:19:22,710 --> 00:19:26,880
And so you can think of--

377
00:19:26,880 --> 00:19:29,820
in this case, you can think of
instead of just maximizing mu,

378
00:19:29,820 --> 00:19:32,280
you could think of
adding a regularizer

379
00:19:32,280 --> 00:19:35,760
to mu that encourages
it to consider

380
00:19:35,760 --> 00:19:38,200
a broader set of actions.

381
00:19:38,200 --> 00:19:40,040
And specifically,
you can think of that

382
00:19:40,040 --> 00:19:43,160
as choosing a mu
that's maximizing Q

383
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:47,880
but also has some entropy to it.

384
00:19:47,880 --> 00:19:51,620
And so if you view this
as adding a regularizer,

385
00:19:51,620 --> 00:19:55,140
then you could choose a
mu that has high entropy,

386
00:19:55,140 --> 00:19:58,720
meaning that it is a
broader distribution.

387
00:19:58,720 --> 00:20:02,960
And it turns out that if you--

388
00:20:02,960 --> 00:20:06,360
for this equation, if you're
trying to choose large Qs

389
00:20:06,360 --> 00:20:11,640
and choose a distribution that
has wider, larger entropy,

390
00:20:11,640 --> 00:20:15,128
then you can
actually write down--

391
00:20:15,128 --> 00:20:17,420
you can write down what this
looks like in closed form.

392
00:20:17,420 --> 00:20:20,520
And in particular, for
maximizing Q and maximizing

393
00:20:20,520 --> 00:20:23,260
entropy, you can show
that the optimal mu

394
00:20:23,260 --> 00:20:27,777
is proportional to the
exponentiated Q values.

395
00:20:27,777 --> 00:20:29,360
Won't go into the
derivation for this.

396
00:20:29,360 --> 00:20:31,860
We possibly will
post something on Ed

397
00:20:31,860 --> 00:20:34,340
after lecture if
you're interested.

398
00:20:34,340 --> 00:20:39,620
And then this term turns
out to simply come out

399
00:20:39,620 --> 00:20:44,980
to a log [? sum ?] x over
your Q values without having

400
00:20:44,980 --> 00:20:48,980
to construct an explicit mu.

401
00:20:48,980 --> 00:20:52,740
And so then the
final objective is

402
00:20:52,740 --> 00:20:54,030
going to look something like--

403
00:20:54,030 --> 00:20:57,820

404
00:20:57,820 --> 00:21:02,140
will look something like the
loss function for the critic

405
00:21:02,140 --> 00:21:12,900
plus the log [? sum ?] x of Q of
(s, a) with an alpha term here

406
00:21:12,900 --> 00:21:20,452
minus alpha times the expected
Q values for states and actions

407
00:21:20,452 --> 00:21:21,160
in your data set.

408
00:21:21,160 --> 00:21:26,320

409
00:21:26,320 --> 00:21:29,240
And you will-- this means
that you don't actually

410
00:21:29,240 --> 00:21:32,420
need to construct mu directly.

411
00:21:32,420 --> 00:21:34,420
This was the only term
that depended on mu.

412
00:21:34,420 --> 00:21:38,923
And we just replaced this
with this term right here.

413
00:21:38,923 --> 00:21:40,840
Yeah, so if there's a
continuous action space,

414
00:21:40,840 --> 00:21:45,600
then you'll want to
sample to estimate this.

415
00:21:45,600 --> 00:21:47,120
OK.

416
00:21:47,120 --> 00:21:48,200
Cool.

417
00:21:48,200 --> 00:21:53,960
So for this algorithm called
CQL or conservative Q-learning,

418
00:21:53,960 --> 00:21:55,480
I figured [INAUDIBLE]
application

419
00:21:55,480 --> 00:21:58,520
of this just as an example.

420
00:21:58,520 --> 00:22:01,600
We've seen a lot of
applications in robotics

421
00:22:01,600 --> 00:22:04,160
and talked about
driving and so forth.

422
00:22:04,160 --> 00:22:06,320
It turns out that
some folks at LinkedIn

423
00:22:06,320 --> 00:22:09,160
use this algorithm
to optimize a policy

424
00:22:09,160 --> 00:22:13,260
for sending notifications,
like email notifications,

425
00:22:13,260 --> 00:22:14,240
for example.

426
00:22:14,240 --> 00:22:16,580
And usually, when you
send notifications,

427
00:22:16,580 --> 00:22:19,842
you want to optimize for
things like how much people

428
00:22:19,842 --> 00:22:22,300
are clicking on it, because
that means that it is something

429
00:22:22,300 --> 00:22:24,100
that they're potentially
interested in,

430
00:22:24,100 --> 00:22:26,620
but also want to minimize
the total number of times

431
00:22:26,620 --> 00:22:28,380
that you're sending
them a notification

432
00:22:28,380 --> 00:22:30,420
so that you're not
spamming the user.

433
00:22:30,420 --> 00:22:33,420
You might also care about
weekly active users.

434
00:22:33,420 --> 00:22:38,620
And they took the
double DQN algorithm.

435
00:22:38,620 --> 00:22:40,440
They also had their
existing system,

436
00:22:40,440 --> 00:22:41,740
which is the baseline here.

437
00:22:41,740 --> 00:22:48,900
And they also tried applying the
CQL terms to a DQN objective.

438
00:22:48,900 --> 00:22:53,380
And they found that when you
added CQL using an offline data

439
00:22:53,380 --> 00:22:58,100
set and then ran that policy
in an online A/B test,

440
00:22:58,100 --> 00:23:01,060
they were able to get a higher
click-through rate, a lower

441
00:23:01,060 --> 00:23:06,120
number of total notifications,
and also a slight increase

442
00:23:06,120 --> 00:23:08,460
in user usage.

443
00:23:08,460 --> 00:23:08,960
So yeah.

444
00:23:08,960 --> 00:23:12,700
This is one example
of a scenario where

445
00:23:12,700 --> 00:23:15,380
offline RL and
specifically this algorithm

446
00:23:15,380 --> 00:23:18,400
was used in a fairly
real-world scenario.

447
00:23:18,400 --> 00:23:20,040
Yeah, so the question
is, why do we

448
00:23:20,040 --> 00:23:22,400
want to be conservative
in the first place?

449
00:23:22,400 --> 00:23:28,240
So this really comes down to
the very initial motivation

450
00:23:28,240 --> 00:23:31,640
for offline RL, which is
that outside of the support

451
00:23:31,640 --> 00:23:37,896
of the data, when you
cannot collect online data,

452
00:23:37,896 --> 00:23:42,760
you can't really learn a policy
that's far beyond the data.

453
00:23:42,760 --> 00:23:46,252
And one reason to
be conservative

454
00:23:46,252 --> 00:23:47,960
is specifically trying
to be conservative

455
00:23:47,960 --> 00:23:51,560
outside the support of
the data, because those

456
00:23:51,560 --> 00:23:53,720
are the regimes where you
don't have the ability

457
00:23:53,720 --> 00:23:54,920
to collect more data.

458
00:23:54,920 --> 00:23:58,720
And if you try to learn--

459
00:23:58,720 --> 00:24:00,980
if you assume that you can
go far beyond the data,

460
00:24:00,980 --> 00:24:03,200
then you might learn an
inaccurate Q function

461
00:24:03,200 --> 00:24:05,350
and, as a result, might
learn a poor policy.

462
00:24:05,350 --> 00:24:08,680

463
00:24:08,680 --> 00:24:09,560
OK.

464
00:24:09,560 --> 00:24:13,760
So to summarize, online
data is expensive.

465
00:24:13,760 --> 00:24:16,380
Using offline data is useful.

466
00:24:16,380 --> 00:24:18,820
A key challenge that
comes up in offline RL

467
00:24:18,820 --> 00:24:21,700
is overestimation because of
the shift between the policy

468
00:24:21,700 --> 00:24:24,783
and the data and the policy
that you're learning.

469
00:24:24,783 --> 00:24:26,700
And we talked about a
few different approaches

470
00:24:26,700 --> 00:24:27,360
for doing this.

471
00:24:27,360 --> 00:24:28,735
And the one we
talked about today

472
00:24:28,735 --> 00:24:33,540
was to try to explicitly
penalize Q values.

473
00:24:33,540 --> 00:24:36,100
And offline RL
methods are allowed--

474
00:24:36,100 --> 00:24:39,220
are able to improve over
imitation learning, including

475
00:24:39,220 --> 00:24:41,500
filtered imitation learning,
by stitching together

476
00:24:41,500 --> 00:24:45,760
good behaviors and leveraging
reward information.

477
00:24:45,760 --> 00:24:48,460

478
00:24:48,460 --> 00:24:49,580
Cool.

479
00:24:49,580 --> 00:24:53,140
So let's shift gears and
talk about reward learning.

480
00:24:53,140 --> 00:24:56,740
So in everything that
we've talked about so far--

481
00:24:56,740 --> 00:24:58,380
well, except for
imitation learning--

482
00:24:58,380 --> 00:25:02,460
we've been trying to
maximize the sum of rewards.

483
00:25:02,460 --> 00:25:03,980
And there's this
question of, where

484
00:25:03,980 --> 00:25:06,280
do the rewards come
from in the first place,

485
00:25:06,280 --> 00:25:08,660
and where do we get them?

486
00:25:08,660 --> 00:25:12,220
And some of the very initial use
cases of reinforcement learning

487
00:25:12,220 --> 00:25:14,230
were with computer
games, where you can just

488
00:25:14,230 --> 00:25:17,910
use the score of the game
as your reward function,

489
00:25:17,910 --> 00:25:22,070
and then get a player that
plays the game very well.

490
00:25:22,070 --> 00:25:23,950
But in real-world
scenarios, you often

491
00:25:23,950 --> 00:25:29,910
don't get rewards as directly,
especially when we're developing

492
00:25:29,910 --> 00:25:30,930
computers to do things.

493
00:25:30,930 --> 00:25:34,950
So if you wanted to develop
a robot that could pour water

494
00:25:34,950 --> 00:25:38,590
from a pitcher into a cup is
fairly complex for a robot

495
00:25:38,590 --> 00:25:42,030
to do, although young children
are able to do it fairly well.

496
00:25:42,030 --> 00:25:43,830
Humans maybe get
some kind of reward

497
00:25:43,830 --> 00:25:47,190
when they drink the water
and some sort of satisfaction

498
00:25:47,190 --> 00:25:48,130
from that.

499
00:25:48,130 --> 00:25:51,130
But robots do not get
that kind of satisfaction.

500
00:25:51,130 --> 00:25:53,070
And so if you were to
try to get a reward

501
00:25:53,070 --> 00:25:55,310
function for this
kind of pouring task,

502
00:25:55,310 --> 00:25:59,910
then you would need to
think about how to represent

503
00:25:59,910 --> 00:26:00,810
where the water is.

504
00:26:00,810 --> 00:26:01,850
Did you spill any water?

505
00:26:01,850 --> 00:26:04,650
Did the water get into
the cup and so forth?

506
00:26:04,650 --> 00:26:08,030
So it's pretty challenging to
get a reward function for that.

507
00:26:08,030 --> 00:26:11,410
If you have some kind
of dialogue situation,

508
00:26:11,410 --> 00:26:13,540
you're trying to
develop a chatbot,

509
00:26:13,540 --> 00:26:15,790
people don't directly tell
you that was a reward of 1.

510
00:26:15,790 --> 00:26:17,170
That was a reward of 10.

511
00:26:17,170 --> 00:26:18,850
And so you need
to think about how

512
00:26:18,850 --> 00:26:22,370
to get a reward function that's
informative for reinforcement

513
00:26:22,370 --> 00:26:23,290
learning.

514
00:26:23,290 --> 00:26:27,050
Likewise, in autonomous driving,
the real world doesn't just

515
00:26:27,050 --> 00:26:31,895
give you reward functions, and
you need to think about how--

516
00:26:31,895 --> 00:26:33,770
a number of different
factors, like the rules

517
00:26:33,770 --> 00:26:40,490
of the road, how comfortable is
it for the user, and so forth.

518
00:26:40,490 --> 00:26:45,850
So in some cases, we can use
a proxy for the true reward.

519
00:26:45,850 --> 00:26:48,850
And you could have a
human giving the rewards

520
00:26:48,850 --> 00:26:53,250
like maybe giving like 1 if that
was successful, 0 otherwise.

521
00:26:53,250 --> 00:26:56,990
You might have some
proxy in terms of things

522
00:26:56,990 --> 00:26:57,990
like click-through rate.

523
00:26:57,990 --> 00:26:58,890
That's a proxy.

524
00:26:58,890 --> 00:27:01,265
We don't necessarily care
about humans clicking on things

525
00:27:01,265 --> 00:27:04,280
inherently, but it's a proxy
for what we might care about.

526
00:27:04,280 --> 00:27:07,890

527
00:27:07,890 --> 00:27:10,450
But even with proxies,
there's a lot of downsides.

528
00:27:10,450 --> 00:27:13,390
And it's worth thinking
about if there's other ways

529
00:27:13,390 --> 00:27:16,030
to provide supervision
to reinforcement learning

530
00:27:16,030 --> 00:27:16,890
algorithms.

531
00:27:16,890 --> 00:27:19,978

532
00:27:19,978 --> 00:27:21,770
We've seen one alternative
approach so far,

533
00:27:21,770 --> 00:27:23,847
which is just to use
imitation learning.

534
00:27:23,847 --> 00:27:25,430
And you don't need
any reward function

535
00:27:25,430 --> 00:27:27,190
if you use imitation learning.

536
00:27:27,190 --> 00:27:28,690
But when you use
imitation learning,

537
00:27:28,690 --> 00:27:31,023
you're not really thinking
about outcomes and optimizing

538
00:27:31,023 --> 00:27:32,090
for a particular outcome.

539
00:27:32,090 --> 00:27:36,212
You're just optimizing
for mimicking the actions.

540
00:27:36,212 --> 00:27:37,670
You might also have
scenarios where

541
00:27:37,670 --> 00:27:39,170
it's hard to provide
demonstrations,

542
00:27:39,170 --> 00:27:41,450
or where the expert has
different degrees of freedom.

543
00:27:41,450 --> 00:27:44,550
That might make it hard
to get good demonstration

544
00:27:44,550 --> 00:27:47,333
data for imitation.

545
00:27:47,333 --> 00:27:48,750
And so a lot of
reward learning is

546
00:27:48,750 --> 00:27:52,470
to think about what is the
underlying outcome that we

547
00:27:52,470 --> 00:27:53,190
want to see.

548
00:27:53,190 --> 00:27:55,710
What is the expert
trying to achieve?

549
00:27:55,710 --> 00:27:57,750
What do humans want?

550
00:27:57,750 --> 00:27:59,250
So people are able
to infer rewards.

551
00:27:59,250 --> 00:28:00,630
There's a few other examples.

552
00:28:00,630 --> 00:28:05,623
Lots of fun videos, where
even very small kids are

553
00:28:05,623 --> 00:28:07,790
able to figure out what is
the reward function, what

554
00:28:07,790 --> 00:28:11,730
is the objective, and
be able to even optimize

555
00:28:11,730 --> 00:28:14,890
that to help the person.

556
00:28:14,890 --> 00:28:15,430
OK.

557
00:28:15,430 --> 00:28:18,490
So how do we learn rewards?

558
00:28:18,490 --> 00:28:20,630
We'll talk about two
approaches for doing this.

559
00:28:20,630 --> 00:28:24,250
One is from examples of
goals, and another one

560
00:28:24,250 --> 00:28:27,250
is from human preferences.

561
00:28:27,250 --> 00:28:34,010
So one of the simplest things
we could consider doing

562
00:28:34,010 --> 00:28:38,850
is try to give examples of
what success looks like,

563
00:28:38,850 --> 00:28:43,530
and then train a classifier to
predict success versus failure.

564
00:28:43,530 --> 00:28:45,610
So, for example, if
we wanted a robot

565
00:28:45,610 --> 00:28:47,570
to put a pencil case
behind a notebook,

566
00:28:47,570 --> 00:28:49,010
we give it a few
examples of what

567
00:28:49,010 --> 00:28:54,250
that looks like, give it a few
negative examples of scenarios

568
00:28:54,250 --> 00:28:55,970
where it's not
behind the notebook,

569
00:28:55,970 --> 00:28:58,170
and then train a
binary classifier

570
00:28:58,170 --> 00:29:02,863
to be able to predict that
the positive examples should

571
00:29:02,863 --> 00:29:04,530
be labeled with 1,
the negative examples

572
00:29:04,530 --> 00:29:07,830
should be labeled with 0.

573
00:29:07,830 --> 00:29:10,710
And then you could use the
output of this binary classifier

574
00:29:10,710 --> 00:29:14,035
as your reward signal.

575
00:29:14,035 --> 00:29:15,910
So specifically, what
this would look like is

576
00:29:15,910 --> 00:29:17,910
you first collect examples
of successful states,

577
00:29:17,910 --> 00:29:19,390
unsuccessful states.

578
00:29:19,390 --> 00:29:22,310
You can think of this as states
within some sort of goal set

579
00:29:22,310 --> 00:29:25,110
and states outside
of that goal set,

580
00:29:25,110 --> 00:29:28,830
and then train a
binary classifier,

581
00:29:28,830 --> 00:29:34,670
and then run reinforcement
learning with this classifier.

582
00:29:34,670 --> 00:29:38,230
Does anyone have thoughts
on what could go wrong

583
00:29:38,230 --> 00:29:40,510
if you took this approach?

584
00:29:40,510 --> 00:29:42,430
Yeah, so one downside
with this approach

585
00:29:42,430 --> 00:29:44,690
is that it's going to give
you a very sparse reward.

586
00:29:44,690 --> 00:29:48,270
It's just going to
tell you, are you

587
00:29:48,270 --> 00:29:51,090
within this set of goal states,
or are you outside of it?

588
00:29:51,090 --> 00:29:52,590
And you would need
to figure out how

589
00:29:52,590 --> 00:29:54,788
to make progress towards that.

590
00:29:54,788 --> 00:29:56,830
Is there any other challenges
or any other things

591
00:29:56,830 --> 00:29:58,402
that might go wrong?

592
00:29:58,402 --> 00:30:00,110
Yeah, so a second
thing that might happen

593
00:30:00,110 --> 00:30:02,210
is it might learn to
fool the classifier,

594
00:30:02,210 --> 00:30:05,250
but not actually do the task
that you're interested in.

595
00:30:05,250 --> 00:30:09,710
And in particular, when
you train a classifier,

596
00:30:09,710 --> 00:30:12,730
it's trained on some
distribution of examples

597
00:30:12,730 --> 00:30:14,050
that you provided.

598
00:30:14,050 --> 00:30:16,810
But the policy that
you're learning

599
00:30:16,810 --> 00:30:19,710
has the ability to visit
lots of different states,

600
00:30:19,710 --> 00:30:23,110
including states that are
different from the initial data

601
00:30:23,110 --> 00:30:24,130
set.

602
00:30:24,130 --> 00:30:26,850
And in some ways, analogous
to some of the challenges

603
00:30:26,850 --> 00:30:28,250
we saw in offline RL.

604
00:30:28,250 --> 00:30:31,330
When you're optimizing
against this classifier,

605
00:30:31,330 --> 00:30:36,050
you can hack it or fool
it and find states where

606
00:30:36,050 --> 00:30:37,930
that classifier is inaccurate.

607
00:30:37,930 --> 00:30:39,810
And when it's inaccurate,
some of the times

608
00:30:39,810 --> 00:30:45,130
it might be optimistic
about how to do the task.

609
00:30:45,130 --> 00:30:48,110
So the first answer
was absolutely correct.

610
00:30:48,110 --> 00:30:50,170
It does provide a sparse signal.

611
00:30:50,170 --> 00:30:52,850
But one especially thing
that can go extra wrong here

612
00:30:52,850 --> 00:30:55,170
is that the RL algorithm
will seek out states

613
00:30:55,170 --> 00:30:57,050
that the classifier
thinks are good.

614
00:30:57,050 --> 00:31:00,170
And it might simply find states
that the classifier wasn't

615
00:31:00,170 --> 00:31:04,900
trained on, and
essentially exploiting

616
00:31:04,900 --> 00:31:08,100
the classifier's weaknesses.

617
00:31:08,100 --> 00:31:12,700
So can we prevent the algorithm
from exploiting the weaknesses?

618
00:31:12,700 --> 00:31:15,440
In some ways, it's
actually nice that we had--

619
00:31:15,440 --> 00:31:17,940
we're talking about this at the
same time in which we talked

620
00:31:17,940 --> 00:31:20,780
about CQL, because we can
actually use an approach that's

621
00:31:20,780 --> 00:31:27,020
very similar to CQL for
preventing the RL algorithm

622
00:31:27,020 --> 00:31:29,540
from exploiting the weaknesses.

623
00:31:29,540 --> 00:31:35,980
And in particular, say that you
have some kind of positive goal

624
00:31:35,980 --> 00:31:39,078
states over here where--

625
00:31:39,078 --> 00:31:39,620
I don't know.

626
00:31:39,620 --> 00:31:41,203
In the pencil case
example, maybe this

627
00:31:41,203 --> 00:31:44,500
is where the pencil
case should be.

628
00:31:44,500 --> 00:31:46,540
You could also just
generally think of it

629
00:31:46,540 --> 00:31:48,220
as some abstract space.

630
00:31:48,220 --> 00:31:52,500
And then you also have
negative examples over here.

631
00:31:52,500 --> 00:31:54,740
Maybe this is a
bad place to go to.

632
00:31:54,740 --> 00:32:00,920
And then your classifier, when
you train a binary classifier,

633
00:32:00,920 --> 00:32:05,280
it will learn some sort of
decision boundary like this.

634
00:32:05,280 --> 00:32:09,800
And then when you
train a policy, when

635
00:32:09,800 --> 00:32:12,000
it sees that the stuff
over here is positive

636
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:14,240
and the stuff over
here is negative,

637
00:32:14,240 --> 00:32:17,640
maybe we can also
specify the direction

638
00:32:17,640 --> 00:32:19,200
of our binary classifier.

639
00:32:19,200 --> 00:32:26,920
Then your RL algorithm, it might
learn to go somewhere over here.

640
00:32:26,920 --> 00:32:30,520
And maybe some of these
states are good, but maybe

641
00:32:30,520 --> 00:32:34,680
also some of these
states are not so good.

642
00:32:34,680 --> 00:32:41,080
And if we want to avoid
exploiting the classifier,

643
00:32:41,080 --> 00:32:43,480
one simple thing
that we could do

644
00:32:43,480 --> 00:32:47,720
is we could take these states
that we visited with our policy,

645
00:32:47,720 --> 00:32:55,240
and we could then label them
as additional data points

646
00:32:55,240 --> 00:32:58,200
for training our classifier.

647
00:32:58,200 --> 00:33:01,140
Now we don't necessarily-- if
we don't have a good reward

648
00:33:01,140 --> 00:33:04,100
function and we don't
want supervision online,

649
00:33:04,100 --> 00:33:07,420
then we don't know exactly
what the labels should be.

650
00:33:07,420 --> 00:33:10,540
And one thing that we could do
is simply to be conservative.

651
00:33:10,540 --> 00:33:15,980
We could label
these as negatives

652
00:33:15,980 --> 00:33:19,060
and say that all of
these were states

653
00:33:19,060 --> 00:33:24,220
that were failures, essentially,
and then retrain our classifier.

654
00:33:24,220 --> 00:33:26,620
And if we retrain
our classifier,

655
00:33:26,620 --> 00:33:34,780
we would then get a classifier
that maybe looks like this.

656
00:33:34,780 --> 00:33:37,840
And then maybe our
policy visits over here.

657
00:33:37,840 --> 00:33:40,340
And then you eventually
learn a classifier

658
00:33:40,340 --> 00:33:43,550
that is more accurate
around these states.

659
00:33:43,550 --> 00:33:46,340

660
00:33:46,340 --> 00:33:47,120
Is there any--

661
00:33:47,120 --> 00:33:47,680
well, OK.

662
00:33:47,680 --> 00:33:51,500
So let's walk through what
this would look explicitly.

663
00:33:51,500 --> 00:33:53,420
So we could add
states that the RL

664
00:33:53,420 --> 00:33:56,825
algorithm visits as negative
examples for the classifier.

665
00:33:56,825 --> 00:33:58,200
So specifically,
we would collect

666
00:33:58,200 --> 00:34:01,760
some initial set of success
states and unsuccessful states.

667
00:34:01,760 --> 00:34:04,160
And then we would
update our classifier

668
00:34:04,160 --> 00:34:08,040
and train it to classify
the success states

669
00:34:08,040 --> 00:34:10,280
and the unsuccessful states.

670
00:34:10,280 --> 00:34:13,159
Then we would collect some
experience using our policy

671
00:34:13,159 --> 00:34:17,719
and update our policy using
the classifier-based reward.

672
00:34:17,719 --> 00:34:22,440
And then we'd add the visited
states to our negative examples,

673
00:34:22,440 --> 00:34:26,480
and then reupdate our classifier
using those negative states.

674
00:34:26,480 --> 00:34:30,639
Does anyone have concerns
about this algorithm?

675
00:34:30,639 --> 00:34:32,880
The concern there is
that you might not

676
00:34:32,880 --> 00:34:34,920
learn a very robust
classifier, and you

677
00:34:34,920 --> 00:34:38,320
might overfit to the
positives in your data set.

678
00:34:38,320 --> 00:34:43,000
And in particular, if you have
a small number of positives,

679
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:50,600
then you might learn a decision
boundary that is very--

680
00:34:50,600 --> 00:34:53,840
only contains each
of these positives

681
00:34:53,840 --> 00:34:56,860
and doesn't contain the
things right around them.

682
00:34:56,860 --> 00:34:59,820
And so one thing that
you can do for that

683
00:34:59,820 --> 00:35:01,600
is to try to regularize
your classifier

684
00:35:01,600 --> 00:35:03,220
to try to prevent
that overfitting.

685
00:35:03,220 --> 00:35:09,520
And even standard regularizers
can be helpful for that.

686
00:35:09,520 --> 00:35:12,700
You can encourage it
to be more smooth.

687
00:35:12,700 --> 00:35:15,460
Any other concerns
about this algorithm?

688
00:35:15,460 --> 00:35:17,883
Yeah, so one of
the challenges that

689
00:35:17,883 --> 00:35:19,300
can come up with
this algorithm is

690
00:35:19,300 --> 00:35:21,550
that you're going to be
adding more and more negatives

691
00:35:21,550 --> 00:35:23,540
to your data set.

692
00:35:23,540 --> 00:35:25,233
And one really
critical thing here

693
00:35:25,233 --> 00:35:27,400
is that if you keep on
adding negatives to your data

694
00:35:27,400 --> 00:35:30,320
set and don't rebalance
your data set,

695
00:35:30,320 --> 00:35:33,020
then it might just predict
negative everywhere.

696
00:35:33,020 --> 00:35:37,840
And so one critical thing here
is to balance the data set 50/50

697
00:35:37,840 --> 00:35:40,940
so that the negatives account
for half of the data that

698
00:35:40,940 --> 00:35:43,500
you're sampling, and the
positives account for the other

699
00:35:43,500 --> 00:35:46,060
half of the data
that you're sampling.

700
00:35:46,060 --> 00:35:48,340
Yeah, so the question
was that at some point

701
00:35:48,340 --> 00:35:50,940
your original negatives will
be overwhelmed by the sample

702
00:35:50,940 --> 00:35:51,760
negatives.

703
00:35:51,760 --> 00:35:54,430
And does it make
sense to balance them?

704
00:35:54,430 --> 00:35:56,510
Certainly, you could do that.

705
00:35:56,510 --> 00:36:01,150
It depends on how large
your initial negatives are.

706
00:36:01,150 --> 00:36:03,213
And so that's
something to consider.

707
00:36:03,213 --> 00:36:04,630
One of the things
that will happen

708
00:36:04,630 --> 00:36:06,750
is that your policy
will actually

709
00:36:06,750 --> 00:36:09,950
start visiting the states here.

710
00:36:09,950 --> 00:36:11,750
And it might visit
like even exactly

711
00:36:11,750 --> 00:36:14,270
the same states
as the positives.

712
00:36:14,270 --> 00:36:18,070
And you're then going to be
labeling these as negatives.

713
00:36:18,070 --> 00:36:22,376
Now those labels are incorrect.

714
00:36:22,376 --> 00:36:26,550
Those states are
actually true positives.

715
00:36:26,550 --> 00:36:29,150
And this also alludes to
the overfitting comment

716
00:36:29,150 --> 00:36:30,950
at the beginning.

717
00:36:30,950 --> 00:36:35,310
But it actually turns out that
if you balance your data set,

718
00:36:35,310 --> 00:36:39,670
this might actually be OK and
if you use enough regularization

719
00:36:39,670 --> 00:36:42,790
or have a large enough
positive and negative data set.

720
00:36:42,790 --> 00:36:45,550
And the reason why
this might be OK

721
00:36:45,550 --> 00:36:54,210
is that when you
use balancing, it

722
00:36:54,210 --> 00:37:00,370
will ensure that the half of
the data set will be positives.

723
00:37:00,370 --> 00:37:05,970
And even if-- let's say that--

724
00:37:05,970 --> 00:37:08,090
OK, so we know that
50% of the data set

725
00:37:08,090 --> 00:37:11,970
is going to be like
true positives.

726
00:37:11,970 --> 00:37:15,982
And then for the other
part of the data set,

727
00:37:15,982 --> 00:37:18,530
some proportion of it
will be false negatives

728
00:37:18,530 --> 00:37:22,210
and some proportion of it
will be true negatives.

729
00:37:22,210 --> 00:37:26,170
Let's say that we've
gotten to the point where

730
00:37:26,170 --> 00:37:32,010
for the other 50%, 40% is
a false negative, meaning

731
00:37:32,010 --> 00:37:35,050
that it's labeled as a negative,
but it's actually a positive.

732
00:37:35,050 --> 00:37:38,330
And 10%, including our
original negatives,

733
00:37:38,330 --> 00:37:43,090
corresponds to true negatives.

734
00:37:43,090 --> 00:37:44,650
Then in this case,
as long as we're

735
00:37:44,650 --> 00:37:53,070
balancing our data set, for a
positive, it will still output--

736
00:37:53,070 --> 00:37:55,990
it won't output
like probability 1

737
00:37:55,990 --> 00:37:59,630
that something is a positive
because of all these here.

738
00:37:59,630 --> 00:38:03,350
But it's still going to output
a probability that something

739
00:38:03,350 --> 00:38:10,430
is positive that's higher
than 50% and probability

740
00:38:10,430 --> 00:38:15,830
that's higher than the
probability that it is negative.

741
00:38:15,830 --> 00:38:21,136
And so, yeah, specifically,
with balancing,

742
00:38:21,136 --> 00:38:24,750
you can always expect
that the probability

743
00:38:24,750 --> 00:38:29,950
of a true positive under
our classifier, this

744
00:38:29,950 --> 00:38:32,630
will always be--

745
00:38:32,630 --> 00:38:34,350
if the classifier is
training properly,

746
00:38:34,350 --> 00:38:38,070
this will always be
greater than 50%,

747
00:38:38,070 --> 00:38:42,150
because as long as this
data set is not like--

748
00:38:42,150 --> 00:38:45,890
if it's all the way like 50--
like entirely false negatives,

749
00:38:45,890 --> 00:38:48,570
then it will be exactly 50%.

750
00:38:48,570 --> 00:38:50,750
But as long as you have
some true negatives around,

751
00:38:50,750 --> 00:38:54,380
including your original
set of negatives,

752
00:38:54,380 --> 00:38:58,350
then it'll be
strictly above 0.5.

753
00:38:58,350 --> 00:39:08,505
And the probability of a true
negative will be less than 0.5.

754
00:39:08,505 --> 00:39:10,130
The question is,
would this be resolved

755
00:39:10,130 --> 00:39:13,970
if we use confidence
intervals instead of 0 or 1?

756
00:39:13,970 --> 00:39:17,970
So you can also use this
probability as the reward

757
00:39:17,970 --> 00:39:20,250
and not threshold it.

758
00:39:20,250 --> 00:39:24,022
And that is also fine.

759
00:39:24,022 --> 00:39:26,565
In terms of
confidence intervals,

760
00:39:26,565 --> 00:39:28,190
that could also
potentially be helpful,

761
00:39:28,190 --> 00:39:30,510
although I haven't
thought too much about it.

762
00:39:30,510 --> 00:39:33,050
And you would need probably a
slightly more sophisticated RL

763
00:39:33,050 --> 00:39:34,290
method.

764
00:39:34,290 --> 00:39:37,370
Yeah, so if you're in a scenario
where you visit a state that's

765
00:39:37,370 --> 00:39:40,090
very hard to distinguish
between them,

766
00:39:40,090 --> 00:39:42,970
then if you have
regularization, it's

767
00:39:42,970 --> 00:39:45,930
likely to label
it as a positive,

768
00:39:45,930 --> 00:39:48,190
because the regularization
will encourage the function

769
00:39:48,190 --> 00:39:49,590
to be smooth.

770
00:39:49,590 --> 00:39:53,030
But it comes down a lot
to how different it is.

771
00:39:53,030 --> 00:39:54,670
Yeah.

772
00:39:54,670 --> 00:39:56,190
So the question
is, would this be

773
00:39:56,190 --> 00:39:59,270
solved if we use a generative
adversarial network where

774
00:39:59,270 --> 00:40:00,750
the classifier is--

775
00:40:00,750 --> 00:40:05,590
the reward is the discriminator
and the generator is the agent?

776
00:40:05,590 --> 00:40:09,310
It turns out that
this actually--

777
00:40:09,310 --> 00:40:11,850
OK, I don't have-- this is
like two slides from now.

778
00:40:11,850 --> 00:40:13,350
But it turns out
that this actually

779
00:40:13,350 --> 00:40:16,030
is exactly a generative
adversarial network.

780
00:40:16,030 --> 00:40:19,150
So you can think
of this classifier

781
00:40:19,150 --> 00:40:21,270
as a discriminator that's
trying to discriminate

782
00:40:21,270 --> 00:40:23,870
between the positive
examples that we gave

783
00:40:23,870 --> 00:40:26,190
and everything that
the policy is visiting.

784
00:40:26,190 --> 00:40:30,430
And the generator in a
generative adversarial network

785
00:40:30,430 --> 00:40:31,973
is the policy.

786
00:40:31,973 --> 00:40:33,390
You don't necessarily
need to know

787
00:40:33,390 --> 00:40:35,807
about exactly what generative
adversarial networks or GANs

788
00:40:35,807 --> 00:40:36,350
are.

789
00:40:36,350 --> 00:40:38,190
But if you do know
what they are,

790
00:40:38,190 --> 00:40:42,083
this is basically
exactly a form of a GAN.

791
00:40:42,083 --> 00:40:44,250
Yeah, so the question is,
after we run a trajectory,

792
00:40:44,250 --> 00:40:45,670
are we seeing what
the true label is?

793
00:40:45,670 --> 00:40:46,990
And the answer to that is no.

794
00:40:46,990 --> 00:40:50,130
So we're assuming that we don't
get any more human supervision

795
00:40:50,130 --> 00:40:52,030
other than the initial
training data set.

796
00:40:52,030 --> 00:40:54,810
Now one other thing
that you can do here

797
00:40:54,810 --> 00:40:58,290
that makes this a little bit
potentially easier is you

798
00:40:58,290 --> 00:41:01,930
could also label some of the
data as positives or negatives

799
00:41:01,930 --> 00:41:04,170
as well, and use
that to continue

800
00:41:04,170 --> 00:41:06,370
to update your classifier.

801
00:41:06,370 --> 00:41:08,230
And so that's also a
very valid approach.

802
00:41:08,230 --> 00:41:10,558
And there's ways to label
only some of the data

803
00:41:10,558 --> 00:41:12,850
so that you don't have to
label every single trajectory

804
00:41:12,850 --> 00:41:14,930
that the policy is doing.

805
00:41:14,930 --> 00:41:17,560
And that could also help
stabilize the training.

806
00:41:17,560 --> 00:41:20,530

807
00:41:20,530 --> 00:41:25,650
One example of using this
kind of approach in robotics

808
00:41:25,650 --> 00:41:29,790
is a paper that took
50 demonstrations.

809
00:41:29,790 --> 00:41:33,770
It used the final
states as successes

810
00:41:33,770 --> 00:41:36,290
for training the classifier.

811
00:41:36,290 --> 00:41:40,250
It initializes a replay buffer
with the demonstrations.

812
00:41:40,250 --> 00:41:42,780
And if you directly
imitate the demonstrations,

813
00:41:42,780 --> 00:41:47,940
the success rate is around 26%,
because 50 demonstrations is not

814
00:41:47,940 --> 00:41:48,597
that much.

815
00:41:48,597 --> 00:41:50,180
And even if you
collect more, it would

816
00:41:50,180 --> 00:41:52,820
be hard to get to 100% success.

817
00:41:52,820 --> 00:41:57,780
And then if instead you use
this initialization and run

818
00:41:57,780 --> 00:42:00,700
RL with a classifier
learned in this way,

819
00:42:00,700 --> 00:42:06,380
then you get a policy that has,
in this case, a 62% success

820
00:42:06,380 --> 00:42:07,020
rate.

821
00:42:07,020 --> 00:42:10,020
Not awesome yet,
but something that's

822
00:42:10,020 --> 00:42:12,060
substantially higher
than the success

823
00:42:12,060 --> 00:42:13,850
rate of imitation learning.

824
00:42:13,850 --> 00:42:16,957

825
00:42:16,957 --> 00:42:19,540
And one thing that we found that
we talked about that was also

826
00:42:19,540 --> 00:42:22,020
we found in this research
is that it's actually

827
00:42:22,020 --> 00:42:24,780
very important to
regularize the classifier

828
00:42:24,780 --> 00:42:30,540
to help it not overfit to
just the positive, the 50

829
00:42:30,540 --> 00:42:33,160
positive examples that were
provided at the beginning.

830
00:42:33,160 --> 00:42:36,740

831
00:42:36,740 --> 00:42:37,240
OK.

832
00:42:37,240 --> 00:42:41,240
And as aside, this
is how GANs work.

833
00:42:41,240 --> 00:42:43,400
The generative
adversarial networks

834
00:42:43,400 --> 00:42:45,420
are a form of generative model.

835
00:42:45,420 --> 00:42:49,560
They've often been used
for image generation.

836
00:42:49,560 --> 00:42:51,480
And the key idea is
to train a classifier

837
00:42:51,480 --> 00:42:55,240
between the real examples,
like the real positives,

838
00:42:55,240 --> 00:42:59,400
and the generated data, like the
states that the policy visits.

839
00:42:59,400 --> 00:43:05,922
And you try to train a
generator that this classifier--

840
00:43:05,922 --> 00:43:07,880
tried to generate such
that it generates things

841
00:43:07,880 --> 00:43:10,600
that the classifier
thinks is real.

842
00:43:10,600 --> 00:43:14,580
GANs have been used in
a variety of places.

843
00:43:14,580 --> 00:43:17,960
Here are some examples of
images generated by a GAN.

844
00:43:17,960 --> 00:43:23,480
Here's a cool video of an
astronaut dancing on Mars,

845
00:43:23,480 --> 00:43:26,878
and later walking his dog
on Mars that also uses

846
00:43:26,878 --> 00:43:28,170
generative adversarial network.

847
00:43:28,170 --> 00:43:32,660

848
00:43:32,660 --> 00:43:33,160
Cool.

849
00:43:33,160 --> 00:43:37,000

850
00:43:37,000 --> 00:43:42,000
Now as a recap, we talked
about training classifiers.

851
00:43:42,000 --> 00:43:43,540
These classifiers
can be exploited

852
00:43:43,540 --> 00:43:45,700
when you optimize against them.

853
00:43:45,700 --> 00:43:50,020
And the solution is that during
the RL process, if we update

854
00:43:50,020 --> 00:43:55,020
the classifier using negatives
visited by the policy, then

855
00:43:55,020 --> 00:43:59,900
you can avoid it
being exploited.

856
00:43:59,900 --> 00:44:01,580
One of the things
that didn't quite

857
00:44:01,580 --> 00:44:04,157
have-- that I wanted to skip
today, because we don't have

858
00:44:04,157 --> 00:44:06,240
too much time and need to
talk about preferences--

859
00:44:06,240 --> 00:44:11,560
but you can also actually learn
not just goal classifiers.

860
00:44:11,560 --> 00:44:14,700
But you can actually learn
full reward functions.

861
00:44:14,700 --> 00:44:18,940
If you use all of the states in
the demo as positive examples

862
00:44:18,940 --> 00:44:21,860
and all of the states in your
policy rollouts as negatives,

863
00:44:21,860 --> 00:44:26,205
then you can show
that that's actually--

864
00:44:26,205 --> 00:44:27,580
we'll be trying
to learn a reward

865
00:44:27,580 --> 00:44:29,500
function over
state distributions

866
00:44:29,500 --> 00:44:30,850
rather than just goal states.

867
00:44:30,850 --> 00:44:34,440

868
00:44:34,440 --> 00:44:34,940
Cool.

869
00:44:34,940 --> 00:44:37,360
On the upside, this is a
very practical framework.

870
00:44:37,360 --> 00:44:40,540
Adversarial training, this
adding these as negatives.

871
00:44:40,540 --> 00:44:42,280
It can be unstable.

872
00:44:42,280 --> 00:44:44,760
There are a variety of
regularization tricks.

873
00:44:44,760 --> 00:44:47,520
You can actually take them just
directly from the GAN literature

874
00:44:47,520 --> 00:44:49,480
and apply them.

875
00:44:49,480 --> 00:44:51,760
And that is quite helpful.

876
00:44:51,760 --> 00:44:53,800
And then one other
big downside here

877
00:44:53,800 --> 00:44:56,840
is that this requires
examples of what the goal is,

878
00:44:56,840 --> 00:44:58,960
like what the
desired behavior is.

879
00:44:58,960 --> 00:45:01,920
And sometimes generating
those examples

880
00:45:01,920 --> 00:45:06,200
can be challenging
or time-consuming.

881
00:45:06,200 --> 00:45:08,430
OK.

882
00:45:08,430 --> 00:45:11,740
So that's learning
from example goals.

883
00:45:11,740 --> 00:45:15,420
Now let's talk about
learning from preferences.

884
00:45:15,420 --> 00:45:18,937

885
00:45:18,937 --> 00:45:20,520
And this is
specifically-- we're going

886
00:45:20,520 --> 00:45:22,280
to talk less about
goal classifiers

887
00:45:22,280 --> 00:45:24,440
and talk more about
scenarios where we don't just

888
00:45:24,440 --> 00:45:27,020
want to reach states
within some goal set,

889
00:45:27,020 --> 00:45:30,280
but we actually want
to potentially learn

890
00:45:30,280 --> 00:45:32,580
like good behavior
versus bad behavior.

891
00:45:32,580 --> 00:45:34,900
And that might
encapsulate something

892
00:45:34,900 --> 00:45:39,340
beyond just reaching
a good state.

893
00:45:39,340 --> 00:45:43,360
And instead of requiring example
demonstrations or example goals

894
00:45:43,360 --> 00:45:47,380
or in addition to that, we
might want to provide feedback

895
00:45:47,380 --> 00:45:50,963
on the full rollout.

896
00:45:50,963 --> 00:45:52,380
Now one option
that we could do is

897
00:45:52,380 --> 00:45:57,020
we could just ask how
good is this trajectory.

898
00:45:57,020 --> 00:46:00,920
So say that you wanted to
start at this blue dot,

899
00:46:00,920 --> 00:46:05,900
and you want to reach the star
as efficiently as possible.

900
00:46:05,900 --> 00:46:08,300
So it's actually hard.

901
00:46:08,300 --> 00:46:11,100
Now another way to
frame this question

902
00:46:11,100 --> 00:46:13,040
is, which trajectory is better?

903
00:46:13,040 --> 00:46:16,140
So who thinks the blue
trajectory is better

904
00:46:16,140 --> 00:46:18,672
than the pink trajectory?

905
00:46:18,672 --> 00:46:19,880
OK, everyone thinks the blue.

906
00:46:19,880 --> 00:46:22,580
So that's a much easier
question to answer, right?

907
00:46:22,580 --> 00:46:25,860
So these kind of
relative preferences

908
00:46:25,860 --> 00:46:30,100
are often easier to provide than
evaluating absolute performance

909
00:46:30,100 --> 00:46:31,540
of a trajectory.

910
00:46:31,540 --> 00:46:34,780
And this is true
for lines in 2D,

911
00:46:34,780 --> 00:46:38,880
but it's also true
in other use cases,

912
00:46:38,880 --> 00:46:42,720
many real-world
use cases as well.

913
00:46:42,720 --> 00:46:45,040
So maybe we can use
these kind of preferences

914
00:46:45,040 --> 00:46:46,390
to get a reward function.

915
00:46:46,390 --> 00:46:49,320

916
00:46:49,320 --> 00:46:53,120
So say that we--

917
00:46:53,120 --> 00:46:55,960
human told us that one
trajectory was better

918
00:46:55,960 --> 00:46:58,480
than another trajectory.

919
00:46:58,480 --> 00:47:02,920
Then probably one way to think
about what they're saying

920
00:47:02,920 --> 00:47:09,000
is that the sum of rewards
for the states in that

921
00:47:09,000 --> 00:47:13,800
trajectory-- so for the states
and actions in the better

922
00:47:13,800 --> 00:47:15,760
trajectory.

923
00:47:15,760 --> 00:47:20,520
This should probably be
larger than the sum of rewards

924
00:47:20,520 --> 00:47:24,920
for the states and actions
in the trajectory that

925
00:47:24,920 --> 00:47:28,120
was said to be worse.

926
00:47:28,120 --> 00:47:31,850
And I'm using W and L to refer
to the winning trajectory

927
00:47:31,850 --> 00:47:33,190
and the losing trajectory.

928
00:47:33,190 --> 00:47:36,130

929
00:47:36,130 --> 00:47:39,568
And maybe we'll
write this as the--

930
00:47:39,568 --> 00:47:41,610
when we say that the
winning trajectory is better

931
00:47:41,610 --> 00:47:43,650
than the losing
trajectory, we'll

932
00:47:43,650 --> 00:47:47,460
write this with this notation.

933
00:47:47,460 --> 00:47:50,250

934
00:47:50,250 --> 00:47:54,970
So any individual preference
label that's binary saying A

935
00:47:54,970 --> 00:47:57,250
is better than B tells us
a little bit of information

936
00:47:57,250 --> 00:47:59,570
about the reward.

937
00:47:59,570 --> 00:48:02,570
And from there, we
want to figure out

938
00:48:02,570 --> 00:48:07,770
how to aggregate a number
of these preference labels

939
00:48:07,770 --> 00:48:09,880
to learn a reward function.

940
00:48:09,880 --> 00:48:11,630
And so we'll assume
that we have some data

941
00:48:11,630 --> 00:48:21,310
set of these pairs where we
have a pair of trajectories.

942
00:48:21,310 --> 00:48:24,010
We are told which one is the
good one and which one is--

943
00:48:24,010 --> 00:48:28,158
or which one is better
than the other one.

944
00:48:28,158 --> 00:48:29,950
And we want to be able
to use this data set

945
00:48:29,950 --> 00:48:33,230
to learn a reward function.

946
00:48:33,230 --> 00:48:37,990
And one perhaps simple way to
do this is we want to model--

947
00:48:37,990 --> 00:48:40,990
we need some way to write
down an objective for a reward

948
00:48:40,990 --> 00:48:42,230
function.

949
00:48:42,230 --> 00:48:45,590
Let's also say that
our reward function--

950
00:48:45,590 --> 00:48:47,290
let's model it as
a neural network.

951
00:48:47,290 --> 00:48:50,790
So our reward function
will have some parameters.

952
00:48:50,790 --> 00:48:53,590
And then you can
think of it as taking

953
00:48:53,590 --> 00:49:03,220
as input a state and action
and outputting a scalar reward

954
00:49:03,220 --> 00:49:03,720
value.

955
00:49:03,720 --> 00:49:06,768

956
00:49:06,768 --> 00:49:09,310
So if you want to write down an
objective for this, one thing

957
00:49:09,310 --> 00:49:10,390
we could do--

958
00:49:10,390 --> 00:49:13,767
let's say that this is
used similar shorthand

959
00:49:13,767 --> 00:49:14,850
to what we've used before.

960
00:49:14,850 --> 00:49:19,630
This is the reward
of the trajectory.

961
00:49:19,630 --> 00:49:21,350
This expression right here.

962
00:49:21,350 --> 00:49:28,170
Then maybe we could say that if
we took the reward of the better

963
00:49:28,170 --> 00:49:34,330
trajectory and subtracted the
reward of the trajectory that's

964
00:49:34,330 --> 00:49:37,950
worse, then we want this
quantity to be positive.

965
00:49:37,950 --> 00:49:41,530

966
00:49:41,530 --> 00:49:43,110
So that's one starting point.

967
00:49:43,110 --> 00:49:45,690
And from there, we could--

968
00:49:45,690 --> 00:49:47,390
we like to do
maximum likelihood.

969
00:49:47,390 --> 00:49:48,410
We like to think
about probabilities

970
00:49:48,410 --> 00:49:50,570
and do maximum likelihood
training in machine learning,

971
00:49:50,570 --> 00:49:51,190
in general.

972
00:49:51,190 --> 00:49:56,810
So we could try to represent the
probability that a trajectory is

973
00:49:56,810 --> 00:49:58,428
better than--

974
00:49:58,428 --> 00:50:00,470
one trajectory is better
than another trajectory.

975
00:50:00,470 --> 00:50:02,450
And so we could do is
we could take this.

976
00:50:02,450 --> 00:50:05,010
We could pass this into
a sigmoid function, which

977
00:50:05,010 --> 00:50:10,250
will squash it between 0 and
1, and then represent that

978
00:50:10,250 --> 00:50:19,930
as the probability that
the winning trajectory is

979
00:50:19,930 --> 00:50:25,210
better than the trajectory
that was marked as inferior.

980
00:50:25,210 --> 00:50:29,390
And then if we did
this, then we could

981
00:50:29,390 --> 00:50:34,490
try to maximize the probability
under our reward function.

982
00:50:34,490 --> 00:50:38,190
So we could try to
then optimize a reward

983
00:50:38,190 --> 00:50:42,750
function such that the prob--

984
00:50:42,750 --> 00:50:46,530
such that you're maximizing
this probability.

985
00:50:46,530 --> 00:50:52,843
So this would be then summed
over the trajectory pairs

986
00:50:52,843 --> 00:50:53,510
in our data set.

987
00:50:53,510 --> 00:50:56,750

988
00:50:56,750 --> 00:51:00,310
And so specifically, we
would take each of the pairs,

989
00:51:00,310 --> 00:51:03,830
take the sum of rewards.

990
00:51:03,830 --> 00:51:06,390
For each of these, subtract
them, take a sigmoid,

991
00:51:06,390 --> 00:51:09,840
and then try to maximize that
by running gradient descent.

992
00:51:09,840 --> 00:51:14,830

993
00:51:14,830 --> 00:51:18,030
It's also worth
mentioning these taus.

994
00:51:18,030 --> 00:51:19,830
You could do it on
a full trajectory,

995
00:51:19,830 --> 00:51:22,790
but in some scenarios,
if a full trajectory

996
00:51:22,790 --> 00:51:29,410
is driving from Palo Alto to San
Francisco or cleaning a kitchen,

997
00:51:29,410 --> 00:51:32,395
then there's many different
subcomponents of that.

998
00:51:32,395 --> 00:51:33,270
And it might be hard.

999
00:51:33,270 --> 00:51:35,290
There might be some parts of
that trajectory that are better

1000
00:51:35,290 --> 00:51:36,730
and some parts that are worse.

1001
00:51:36,730 --> 00:51:38,982
And so you could also do
this on partial trajectories

1002
00:51:38,982 --> 00:51:40,690
and say like this part
of this trajectory

1003
00:51:40,690 --> 00:51:43,370
is better than this part.

1004
00:51:43,370 --> 00:51:46,250
So you don't have to do
it on full trajectories.

1005
00:51:46,250 --> 00:51:52,530
We want to reward such that the
sum of rewards for the better

1006
00:51:52,530 --> 00:51:55,570
trajectory is higher than the
sum of the rewards for the worst

1007
00:51:55,570 --> 00:51:57,268
trajectory.

1008
00:51:57,268 --> 00:51:59,310
Humans are classifying
which trajectory's better.

1009
00:51:59,310 --> 00:52:01,930
We want the reward to be
discriminative of which

1010
00:52:01,930 --> 00:52:05,010
trajectory is better.

1011
00:52:05,010 --> 00:52:08,450
And so we define
this probability

1012
00:52:08,450 --> 00:52:11,290
that one trajectory is better
than another trajectory.

1013
00:52:11,290 --> 00:52:13,130
And then with this
probability, we

1014
00:52:13,130 --> 00:52:17,490
can try to maximize the
reward parameters such

1015
00:52:17,490 --> 00:52:19,170
that this is true.

1016
00:52:19,170 --> 00:52:20,890
Yeah, so the question
was the human

1017
00:52:20,890 --> 00:52:23,700
isn't telling us how much
better a trajectory is.

1018
00:52:23,700 --> 00:52:27,100
And you could have a scenario
where maybe one trajectory is

1019
00:52:27,100 --> 00:52:31,780
like this, one
trajectory is like this,

1020
00:52:31,780 --> 00:52:34,900
and then a third
trajectory is like--

1021
00:52:34,900 --> 00:52:37,660
I don't know-- going all the
way over here or something.

1022
00:52:37,660 --> 00:52:43,340
And this objective isn't
taking into account

1023
00:52:43,340 --> 00:52:46,260
the fact that the
preference of this over this

1024
00:52:46,260 --> 00:52:49,530
is a smaller preference than the
preference of this over this.

1025
00:52:49,530 --> 00:52:52,532

1026
00:52:52,532 --> 00:52:55,180
When you end up
optimizing this objective,

1027
00:52:55,180 --> 00:52:57,860
oftentimes, it will
actually turn out

1028
00:52:57,860 --> 00:53:01,140
that it will assign something.

1029
00:53:01,140 --> 00:53:03,840
Well, so I guess a
couple of things here.

1030
00:53:03,840 --> 00:53:07,940
So if you have a
lot of data, then it

1031
00:53:07,940 --> 00:53:14,740
will also tell you that
this, the solid green one,

1032
00:53:14,740 --> 00:53:17,300
is better than this one.

1033
00:53:17,300 --> 00:53:21,360
And so you could actually get
lots of pairwise preferences

1034
00:53:21,360 --> 00:53:22,060
on them.

1035
00:53:22,060 --> 00:53:24,733
And so because of those
pairwise preferences,

1036
00:53:24,733 --> 00:53:26,900
you'll see a ranking, like
this is better than this.

1037
00:53:26,900 --> 00:53:27,600
This is better than this.

1038
00:53:27,600 --> 00:53:28,642
This is better than this.

1039
00:53:28,642 --> 00:53:31,320
And when you do that, that'll
force the reward function

1040
00:53:31,320 --> 00:53:33,960
to assign a much higher
reward value to this one

1041
00:53:33,960 --> 00:53:35,600
compared to this one.

1042
00:53:35,600 --> 00:53:39,320
So it will already
do what you want.

1043
00:53:39,320 --> 00:53:42,840
You could also-- there's
a couple other ways

1044
00:53:42,840 --> 00:53:44,260
to get this information.

1045
00:53:44,260 --> 00:53:48,500
Sometimes when you actually
ask humans for preferences,

1046
00:53:48,500 --> 00:53:50,280
sometimes you also
just give them a batch

1047
00:53:50,280 --> 00:53:52,140
and tell them to rank as well.

1048
00:53:52,140 --> 00:53:58,680
And that would make
this even more explicit.

1049
00:53:58,680 --> 00:54:01,440
So the complete algorithm for
what this looks like is you

1050
00:54:01,440 --> 00:54:03,560
have some data set
of trajectories.

1051
00:54:03,560 --> 00:54:06,880
You're going to sample
batches of k trajectories.

1052
00:54:06,880 --> 00:54:09,820
And then ask a human to
rank those k trajectories.

1053
00:54:09,820 --> 00:54:12,840

1054
00:54:12,840 --> 00:54:15,240
For language models
when you do this-- so

1055
00:54:15,240 --> 00:54:17,600
this is actually a common
way to learn reward functions

1056
00:54:17,600 --> 00:54:18,980
for language models.

1057
00:54:18,980 --> 00:54:23,980
And you'll often actually use
the same prompt and sample k

1058
00:54:23,980 --> 00:54:27,780
responses and then ask them
to rank those k responses.

1059
00:54:27,780 --> 00:54:31,660
And this is useful because
these trajectories all

1060
00:54:31,660 --> 00:54:33,880
start from some initial point.

1061
00:54:33,880 --> 00:54:36,180
You can think of this as
starting from the same prompt.

1062
00:54:36,180 --> 00:54:37,597
But you might have
other responses

1063
00:54:37,597 --> 00:54:40,940
that are like starting from
a different prompt over here.

1064
00:54:40,940 --> 00:54:44,080
And for a different prompt or
from a different starting place,

1065
00:54:44,080 --> 00:54:47,100
it's a lot harder to talk
about like, is this better--

1066
00:54:47,100 --> 00:54:49,280
like, is this black one
better than this green one?

1067
00:54:49,280 --> 00:54:51,420
It got there-- maybe
got here faster,

1068
00:54:51,420 --> 00:54:55,825
but it also had-- it was
an easier starting point.

1069
00:54:55,825 --> 00:54:57,885
And so oftentimes,
we'll try to sample

1070
00:54:57,885 --> 00:54:59,260
a batch of
trajectories that have

1071
00:54:59,260 --> 00:55:03,140
a similar starting position.

1072
00:55:03,140 --> 00:55:06,680
Then we will compute the rewards
under our current reward model,

1073
00:55:06,680 --> 00:55:08,740
essentially do a
forward pass, and sum

1074
00:55:08,740 --> 00:55:10,540
over the states and actions.

1075
00:55:10,540 --> 00:55:12,600
And then we can
compute the gradient,

1076
00:55:12,600 --> 00:55:16,700
do a backward pass
using our objective,

1077
00:55:16,700 --> 00:55:22,320
where this is for
every pair of--

1078
00:55:22,320 --> 00:55:25,520
for each pair
within the ranking.

1079
00:55:25,520 --> 00:55:30,360
So there's k choose 2 different
combinations of pairs.

1080
00:55:30,360 --> 00:55:34,520
And if you have a ranking, then
you'll have a full set of k

1081
00:55:34,520 --> 00:55:35,310
choose 2 labels.

1082
00:55:35,310 --> 00:55:38,560

1083
00:55:38,560 --> 00:55:42,000
Then you can update theta
using the computed gradient.

1084
00:55:42,000 --> 00:55:44,670
And then you can also
repeat this process.

1085
00:55:44,670 --> 00:55:48,640
And repeating this just
corresponds to doing

1086
00:55:48,640 --> 00:55:52,040
gradient descent on your
objective on a single data set.

1087
00:55:52,040 --> 00:55:55,320

1088
00:55:55,320 --> 00:55:57,040
Now one thing that
you can also do

1089
00:55:57,040 --> 00:56:01,840
is you can also do this in the
loop of reinforcement learning.

1090
00:56:01,840 --> 00:56:07,080
So you have your samples
from your policy.

1091
00:56:07,080 --> 00:56:13,320
And then you can ask for humans
to rank them and then update

1092
00:56:13,320 --> 00:56:14,640
your rewards.

1093
00:56:14,640 --> 00:56:19,660
And then after you have
your updated reward model,

1094
00:56:19,660 --> 00:56:21,800
then maybe your reward
model is more accurate.

1095
00:56:21,800 --> 00:56:26,260
And maybe you want to be going
like here, for example, instead

1096
00:56:26,260 --> 00:56:27,380
of going here.

1097
00:56:27,380 --> 00:56:31,540
Then you could then reupdate
your reward function

1098
00:56:31,540 --> 00:56:34,220
and then update your policy
again, and then get new rollouts

1099
00:56:34,220 --> 00:56:35,240
for your policy.

1100
00:56:35,240 --> 00:56:37,380
Ask a human to label
those rollouts,

1101
00:56:37,380 --> 00:56:39,350
reupdate your reward
function, and repeat.

1102
00:56:39,350 --> 00:56:42,620

1103
00:56:42,620 --> 00:56:44,340
OK.

1104
00:56:44,340 --> 00:56:48,326
So that's how we can
learn from preferences.

1105
00:56:48,326 --> 00:56:49,640
There's a few examples of this.

1106
00:56:49,640 --> 00:56:50,580
So the first time--

1107
00:56:50,580 --> 00:56:51,955
or some of the
first times people

1108
00:56:51,955 --> 00:56:56,480
have learned from preferences
were in some simulated settings.

1109
00:56:56,480 --> 00:57:00,500
So in this case, if you want
to get this simulated thing

1110
00:57:00,500 --> 00:57:01,980
to do a backflip.

1111
00:57:01,980 --> 00:57:05,820
It's very hard to write a reward
function for doing a backflip.

1112
00:57:05,820 --> 00:57:09,640
And so they used 900 queries.

1113
00:57:09,640 --> 00:57:12,902
They asked humans 900 times
like, is this better than this?

1114
00:57:12,902 --> 00:57:15,360
They actually did it in the
loop of reinforcement learning.

1115
00:57:15,360 --> 00:57:19,445
And they got a policy
that is able to learn

1116
00:57:19,445 --> 00:57:21,320
how to do a backflip,
and in the process also

1117
00:57:21,320 --> 00:57:28,320
learn a reward function for
a human-preferred backflip.

1118
00:57:28,320 --> 00:57:31,220
Also, in driving, it's very
hard to get reward functions.

1119
00:57:31,220 --> 00:57:33,720
And so there was work that
used this kind of approach

1120
00:57:33,720 --> 00:57:36,080
to learn things
like how much does

1121
00:57:36,080 --> 00:57:38,120
it matter to stay
within your lane,

1122
00:57:38,120 --> 00:57:40,463
keep a certain speed,
collision avoidance,

1123
00:57:40,463 --> 00:57:41,380
those sorts of things.

1124
00:57:41,380 --> 00:57:43,840
And you would imagine
that collision avoidance

1125
00:57:43,840 --> 00:57:48,260
is much, much more important
than keeping a certain speed,

1126
00:57:48,260 --> 00:57:49,600
for example.

1127
00:57:49,600 --> 00:57:52,300
And you can learn a
reward function that way.

1128
00:57:52,300 --> 00:57:52,800
Cool.

1129
00:57:52,800 --> 00:57:54,420
And then there's
also language models.

1130
00:57:54,420 --> 00:57:56,800
So this has been
a very popular way

1131
00:57:56,800 --> 00:57:58,580
to learn rewards
for language models.

1132
00:57:58,580 --> 00:58:01,880
And you can have some prompt
like, how do I cook an omelet?

1133
00:58:01,880 --> 00:58:04,080
Then you can sample
from your language model

1134
00:58:04,080 --> 00:58:07,600
two different responses
for that prompt.

1135
00:58:07,600 --> 00:58:11,817
Then we ask a person
which response is better.

1136
00:58:11,817 --> 00:58:13,650
When we're doing training,
we might actually

1137
00:58:13,650 --> 00:58:16,050
pay people to label our data.

1138
00:58:16,050 --> 00:58:17,170
You might have also--

1139
00:58:17,170 --> 00:58:18,630
if you've interacted
with ChatGPT,

1140
00:58:18,630 --> 00:58:20,963
sometimes it might have asked
you these sorts of things.

1141
00:58:20,963 --> 00:58:25,610
You're providing labels
to give OpenAI the ability

1142
00:58:25,610 --> 00:58:31,210
to learn a reward function that
is better for your preferences.

1143
00:58:31,210 --> 00:58:33,630
And then once you have the
things that are better,

1144
00:58:33,630 --> 00:58:35,130
then you can train
a reward model

1145
00:58:35,130 --> 00:58:38,300
that judges how good a response
y is for a given prompt.

1146
00:58:38,300 --> 00:58:42,250

1147
00:58:42,250 --> 00:58:46,890
And so then in terms of what
the kind of full language model

1148
00:58:46,890 --> 00:58:51,130
pipeline is, if you wanted to
train something like ChatGPT,

1149
00:58:51,130 --> 00:58:53,370
first, you'll do some
large-scale pre-training

1150
00:58:53,370 --> 00:59:00,850
on very, very large
mixed quality data.

1151
00:59:00,850 --> 00:59:02,750
With just predicting
the next token,

1152
00:59:02,750 --> 00:59:04,610
then you typically
have a second stage,

1153
00:59:04,610 --> 00:59:08,530
which is to fine-tune
on higher quality data.

1154
00:59:08,530 --> 00:59:12,870
And in particular,
after step one,

1155
00:59:12,870 --> 00:59:15,990
before you do any kind
of supervised fine-tuning

1156
00:59:15,990 --> 00:59:17,390
or reinforcement
learning, you'll

1157
00:59:17,390 --> 00:59:20,070
get a reward, a language model
that knows a lot of things,

1158
00:59:20,070 --> 00:59:22,030
but is not easy to
control or to get it

1159
00:59:22,030 --> 00:59:23,593
to do what you want it to do.

1160
00:59:23,593 --> 00:59:25,010
And it might not
answer questions.

1161
00:59:25,010 --> 00:59:26,210
It might not do
what you requested.

1162
00:59:26,210 --> 00:59:28,190
It might just predict
reasonable next tokens

1163
00:59:28,190 --> 00:59:29,630
that come after that.

1164
00:59:29,630 --> 00:59:31,788
Whereas in supervised
fine-tuning,

1165
00:59:31,788 --> 00:59:33,330
we can generate a
higher quality data

1166
00:59:33,330 --> 00:59:36,910
set that has for a given prompt,
this is how you should reply.

1167
00:59:36,910 --> 00:59:39,030
And so we do some
kind of imitation

1168
00:59:39,030 --> 00:59:41,730
learning on that data set.

1169
00:59:41,730 --> 00:59:43,230
And then there's a
third step, which

1170
00:59:43,230 --> 00:59:46,310
is to use the human
feedback and then fine-tune

1171
00:59:46,310 --> 00:59:49,270
the model that you got
from supervised fine-tuning

1172
00:59:49,270 --> 00:59:53,597
on higher quality
preference data.

1173
00:59:53,597 --> 00:59:55,430
And what this third
step looks like is first

1174
00:59:55,430 --> 00:59:57,230
you gather the preference data.

1175
00:59:57,230 --> 00:59:59,290
There are also actually
existing preference data

1176
00:59:59,290 --> 01:00:00,862
sets that you can use as well.

1177
01:00:00,862 --> 01:00:03,070
You don't have to necessarily--
you don't necessarily

1178
01:00:03,070 --> 01:00:08,730
have to collect it
on using your model.

1179
01:00:08,730 --> 01:00:11,570
Second, you'll
train a reward model

1180
01:00:11,570 --> 01:00:15,183
that is judging how
good a response is.

1181
01:00:15,183 --> 01:00:16,850
And then lastly,
you'll do reinforcement

1182
01:00:16,850 --> 01:00:19,810
learning to maximize
that reward function.

1183
01:00:19,810 --> 01:00:22,290
We will talk more about
preference learning on Wednesday

1184
01:00:22,290 --> 01:00:23,450
next week.

1185
01:00:23,450 --> 01:00:26,010
But one somewhat
common approach is

1186
01:00:26,010 --> 01:00:30,490
to use PPO to
maximize the reward.

1187
01:00:30,490 --> 01:00:32,930
The question is, is
x just the prompt,

1188
01:00:32,930 --> 01:00:34,890
or is it the whole history?

1189
01:00:34,890 --> 01:00:37,290
Oftentimes, it's
the whole history.

1190
01:00:37,290 --> 01:00:40,845
And you can sort of--

1191
01:00:40,845 --> 01:00:42,490
yeah, oftentimes,
when you-- generally,

1192
01:00:42,490 --> 01:00:44,950
also when you formulate RL
problems for language models,

1193
01:00:44,950 --> 01:00:47,830
you will have--

1194
01:00:47,830 --> 01:00:50,490
you can model it as
a sequential problem.

1195
01:00:50,490 --> 01:00:53,530
But you can also--

1196
01:00:53,530 --> 01:00:55,050
there's a way to--

1197
01:00:55,050 --> 01:00:57,017
a simpler but more
crude way to model

1198
01:00:57,017 --> 01:00:58,850
it, which is to use all
the history and just

1199
01:00:58,850 --> 01:01:00,690
treat the next
response as the thing

1200
01:01:00,690 --> 01:01:03,010
that you're optimizing over.

1201
01:01:03,010 --> 01:01:05,570
So the question is, how do we
go from preference rankings

1202
01:01:05,570 --> 01:01:07,950
to this probability right here?

1203
01:01:07,950 --> 01:01:10,790
So in our preferences,
we're saying

1204
01:01:10,790 --> 01:01:13,550
that we might have
a ranking saying

1205
01:01:13,550 --> 01:01:16,370
like A is better than B
which is better than C.

1206
01:01:16,370 --> 01:01:19,830
If we have a ranking
of k trajectories,

1207
01:01:19,830 --> 01:01:24,950
then we can formulate k
choose 2 pairs where--

1208
01:01:24,950 --> 01:01:28,570
and within each pair we'll
have an A is better than B,

1209
01:01:28,570 --> 01:01:30,070
or B is better than A.

1210
01:01:30,070 --> 01:01:35,510
We're using tau w to represent
the response or the trajectory

1211
01:01:35,510 --> 01:01:37,350
that was preferred,
and tau l to represent

1212
01:01:37,350 --> 01:01:38,990
the one that was dispreferred.

1213
01:01:38,990 --> 01:01:44,030
And then we sample one of
those pairs, one of those k

1214
01:01:44,030 --> 01:01:45,190
choose pairs.

1215
01:01:45,190 --> 01:01:48,710
And then we plug it into
this equation right here.

1216
01:01:48,710 --> 01:01:51,030
So you're asking, what is
the sigma function or--?

1217
01:01:51,030 --> 01:01:54,270
yeah, so the sigma
function here--

1218
01:01:54,270 --> 01:01:55,910
this is a sigmoid.

1219
01:01:55,910 --> 01:02:01,110
So it is basically a
function that squashes

1220
01:02:01,110 --> 01:02:03,750
the values between 0 and 1.

1221
01:02:03,750 --> 01:02:07,490
And so it's just a very
simple function that--

1222
01:02:07,490 --> 01:02:08,270
I can't remember.

1223
01:02:08,270 --> 01:02:15,050
I think it looks something
like this, where this is 1.

1224
01:02:15,050 --> 01:02:17,070
So yeah, I'm just using sigma.

1225
01:02:17,070 --> 01:02:18,950
This is sigma right here.

1226
01:02:18,950 --> 01:02:21,690
So it's just this
function right here.

1227
01:02:21,690 --> 01:02:22,410
OK.

1228
01:02:22,410 --> 01:02:26,530
One other thing you can do is
instead of using human feedback,

1229
01:02:26,530 --> 01:02:28,730
you could also have an AI model.

1230
01:02:28,730 --> 01:02:32,070
You could ask a strong model
like, is this better than this?

1231
01:02:32,070 --> 01:02:35,370

1232
01:02:35,370 --> 01:02:37,410
One thing that I'll
note is when you

1233
01:02:37,410 --> 01:02:40,530
are thinking about what is
preferred and dispreferred,

1234
01:02:40,530 --> 01:02:42,330
there are a variety
of different axes

1235
01:02:42,330 --> 01:02:45,330
in which something might be
preferred or dispreferred.

1236
01:02:45,330 --> 01:02:46,370
And so you could--

1237
01:02:46,370 --> 01:02:49,250
in some cases, you might be
able to ask for certain things

1238
01:02:49,250 --> 01:02:52,365
that you care about
from the model.

1239
01:02:52,365 --> 01:02:54,490
This is often referred to
as reinforcement learning

1240
01:02:54,490 --> 01:02:56,570
with AI feedback.

1241
01:02:56,570 --> 01:03:01,810
As one example of a
use case, the people

1242
01:03:01,810 --> 01:03:03,750
have used this to
try to prevent models

1243
01:03:03,750 --> 01:03:07,630
from producing harmful content.

1244
01:03:07,630 --> 01:03:11,510
And so they will feed two
responses into the model

1245
01:03:11,510 --> 01:03:15,750
and ask the model which
response is less harmful.

1246
01:03:15,750 --> 01:03:18,150
One insight here
is that critique is

1247
01:03:18,150 --> 01:03:20,255
often easier than generation.

1248
01:03:20,255 --> 01:03:21,630
And so this is
one of the reasons

1249
01:03:21,630 --> 01:03:26,390
why you might be able to get a
stronger model with AI feedback

1250
01:03:26,390 --> 01:03:27,550
than otherwise.

1251
01:03:27,550 --> 01:03:30,150
And there have been
some works that

1252
01:03:30,150 --> 01:03:34,450
show that if you use this kind
of feedback, this AI feedback,

1253
01:03:34,450 --> 01:03:38,070
you can get an improvement
in model performance

1254
01:03:38,070 --> 01:03:39,480
on different axes.

1255
01:03:39,480 --> 01:03:42,950

1256
01:03:42,950 --> 01:03:43,936
OK.

1257
01:03:43,936 --> 01:03:45,990
So a summary of
reward learning--

1258
01:03:45,990 --> 01:03:49,590
rewards cannot be
taken for granted.

1259
01:03:49,590 --> 01:03:51,370
We've talked about
learning from goals.

1260
01:03:51,370 --> 01:03:54,190
We talked about learning
from human preferences.

1261
01:03:54,190 --> 01:03:56,990
For goals, it's quite practical.

1262
01:03:56,990 --> 01:04:00,640
Adversarial training can be a
little bit tricky to optimize,

1263
01:04:00,640 --> 01:04:03,520
and it requires examples
of desired behavior.

1264
01:04:03,520 --> 01:04:05,400
With preferences,
pairwise preferences

1265
01:04:05,400 --> 01:04:06,820
are really easy to provide.

1266
01:04:06,820 --> 01:04:10,680
It doesn't require example
goals or example demonstrations.

1267
01:04:10,680 --> 01:04:13,680
It has been deployed at
really, really large scales

1268
01:04:13,680 --> 01:04:15,480
with language models.

1269
01:04:15,480 --> 01:04:18,000
It may require some
supervision in the loop

1270
01:04:18,000 --> 01:04:19,550
of reinforcement learning.

1271
01:04:19,550 --> 01:04:22,347

1272
01:04:22,347 --> 01:04:24,680
One other thing that I encourage
everyone to think about

1273
01:04:24,680 --> 01:04:29,320
is goals and human preferences,
those are some form of feedback,

1274
01:04:29,320 --> 01:04:31,320
but there's a lot of
other ways that we

1275
01:04:31,320 --> 01:04:35,000
might be able to provide
supervision to agents

1276
01:04:35,000 --> 01:04:37,000
that are trying to learn.

1277
01:04:37,000 --> 01:04:39,120
You could even verbally
tell it some information

1278
01:04:39,120 --> 01:04:41,120
like, that was good,
or that was helpful,

1279
01:04:41,120 --> 01:04:45,840
or that was helpful but
mean, or something like that.

1280
01:04:45,840 --> 01:04:48,460
And you could think of other
ways of providing supervision.

1281
01:04:48,460 --> 01:04:52,465
And I think that this is
an area of research that--

1282
01:04:52,465 --> 01:04:53,840
yeah-- where I
think that there's

1283
01:04:53,840 --> 01:04:56,000
more things that could be done.

1284
01:04:56,000 --> 01:04:57,480
One other thing
that I'll mention

1285
01:04:57,480 --> 01:05:01,260
is there's also some cool work
in the reinforcement learning

1286
01:05:01,260 --> 01:05:05,110
research landscape,
where you can potentially

1287
01:05:05,110 --> 01:05:06,860
have reinforcement
learning agents propose

1288
01:05:06,860 --> 01:05:11,540
their own goals rather than
having a human provide a goal.

1289
01:05:11,540 --> 01:05:14,660
This is often referred
to as this area

1290
01:05:14,660 --> 01:05:17,780
of unsupervised reinforcement
learning, where human is not

1291
01:05:17,780 --> 01:05:19,920
telling the agent
what it should do.

1292
01:05:19,920 --> 01:05:24,380
Instead, the agent should have
its own sort of curiosity.

1293
01:05:24,380 --> 01:05:26,460
One example of this
is you can have

1294
01:05:26,460 --> 01:05:29,020
one agent setting a goal,
another agent trying

1295
01:05:29,020 --> 01:05:30,380
to reach the goal.

1296
01:05:30,380 --> 01:05:32,820
And you can formulate this
as a game between these two

1297
01:05:32,820 --> 01:05:34,980
different agents.

1298
01:05:34,980 --> 01:05:40,220
And there's, yeah,
some cool work that

1299
01:05:40,220 --> 01:05:42,540
shows that you can have that--

1300
01:05:42,540 --> 01:05:44,700
if you have these two
different agents that

1301
01:05:44,700 --> 01:05:47,540
are trying to play against each
other, where one is the goal

1302
01:05:47,540 --> 01:05:49,460
setter, one is trying
to reach the goals,

1303
01:05:49,460 --> 01:05:53,310
you can get some kind of
cool behavior as a result.

1304
01:05:53,310 --> 01:05:58,000

